// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 cet6（由 build/passages/cet6.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("cet6",{"1":{"w":["abbreviation","abide","abolish","abrupt","absorption","absurd","abundance","accessible","accomplishment","acquaint","acquisitive","addict","adjacent","adjoin","adjustable","admiration","adolescent","adoption","adore","advantageous"],"title":"The Library Next Door","t":"When I was an **adolescent**, the most important place in my life was the small public library **adjacent** to my school. It occupied two rooms that **adjoin** an old post office, and it was open until nine every evening. For a shy teenager, it offered an **abundance** of books and a quiet place to escape.\n\nI was a reading **addict**. I read everything I could find, from adventure novels to science magazines, and my **absorption** in books was so complete that I often forgot to go home for dinner. The librarian, Mrs. Ren, was the first adult I truly regarded with **admiration**. She seemed to know every book in the building, and she would **acquaint** new readers with authors they had never heard of.\n\nThe library had strict rules, and everyone had to **abide** by them: no food, no phones and no talking above a whisper. Some rules seemed **absurd** to us. Library cards, for example, carried a strange **abbreviation** that nobody understood, and late fees were so high that poor students avoided borrowing books at all.\n\nMrs. Ren worked hard to change this. She persuaded the city council to **abolish** late fees and to make the library more **accessible** to children from poor families. She also pushed for the **adoption** of a new computer system and bought **adjustable** chairs and desks for readers of all ages. Each change was a small **accomplishment**, but together they transformed the place.\n\nHer retirement was **abrupt**; one spring she fell ill and left without a farewell party. Many of us were heartbroken, and we still **adore** her today. Looking back, I realise how **advantageous** those years were for me. In a world that often seems **acquisitive**, obsessed with owning more, the library taught me that the most valuable things can be borrowed for free."},"2":{"w":["adverse","aerial","aerospace","aesthetic","affective","affiliate","affirm","afflict","affluence","aggravate","aggression","agitation","agreeable","alas","album","alien","alignment","allegation","allege","allegiance"],"title":"Pages from an Engineer's Album","t":"My aunt is an engineer in the **aerospace** industry, and she keeps a thick photo **album** of her career. It contains **aerial** photographs of rocket launches, pictures of satellites under construction and a few photos of her younger self smiling beside giant machines. For her, the album has great **affective** value: every image brings back strong feelings.\n\nShe grew up in a poor village, and the **affluence** of her life today still surprises her. As a student, she felt like an **alien** in the big city, where everyone seemed richer and more confident. But she had a talent for mathematics, and she was soon recruited by a research institute **affiliated|affiliate** with a top university.\n\nHer work requires extreme precision. A tiny error in the **alignment** of a satellite's solar panels can cause serious problems in space. Engineers must also consider the **aesthetic** design of public displays, she jokes, because politicians like rockets that look impressive.\n\nHer career has not been free of difficulty. Ten years ago, a colleague made an **allegation** that her team had hidden test failures. Reporters began to **allege** that the whole project was unsafe, and some of them behaved with surprising **aggression**, waiting outside her home at night. The **agitation** lasted for months. She felt **afflicted|afflict** by constant stress, and the pressure began to **aggravate** an old stomach problem.\n\nThroughout the investigation, she continued to **affirm** that her team had followed every rule. Her **allegiance** was always to the truth, not to her employer. In the end, investigators found no evidence against her, and the **adverse** publicity disappeared. \"**Alas**, the newspapers never printed an apology,\" she says with a smile. Today she is a calm and **agreeable** woman who rarely mentions those months, though one page of her album is left blank in their memory."},"3":{"w":["alleviate","alliance","allied","allocate","allowance","ally","alongside","alteration","alternate","amateur","ambassador","ambient","ambiguity","ambiguous","ambitious","amends","amid","ample","amplitude","amusement"],"title":"The Amateur Orchestra","t":"Every Thursday evening, about forty people meet in a school hall to play music together. They are an **amateur** orchestra: their members include teachers, doctors, shop owners and retired engineers, and none of them is paid. For many, the weekly rehearsal is the best way to **alleviate** the stress of daily life.\n\nThe orchestra began as an **alliance** between two local schools that wanted to share instruments and teachers. Over time, other **allied** groups joined, including a community choir and a dance club. The city council was an important **ally**, agreeing to **allocate** a small budget for sheet music and a monthly **allowance** for the conductor.\n\nThe conductor, Mr. Qiu, is **ambitious**. He wants the orchestra to perform a full symphony next year, and he works hard **alongside** the players to improve their skills. His instructions, however, are sometimes **ambiguous**. When he says \"play it more softly but with more feeling,\" players are often confused. To avoid **ambiguity**, the first violinist now writes his comments directly on the music.\n\nRehearsals are not always smooth. **Amid** the noise of traffic outside, some players struggle to hear each other, and the **ambient** sound in the old hall is poor. A physics teacher in the orchestra once explained how the **amplitude** of sound waves affects volume, and the group made an **alteration** to its seating plan so that quieter instruments sit closer to the audience.\n\nThe orchestra performs on **alternate** Sundays in a local park. There is **ample** space for families, and children dance with obvious **amusement**. Last year a visiting **ambassador** from Austria attended a concert and praised the players warmly. When a trumpeter played a wrong note during the anthem, he made **amends** by buying everyone coffee afterwards."},"4":{"w":["analogous","analogue","analogy","analyst","analytic","analytical","anniversary","annually","anode","anonymous","antarctic","antenna","antique","antisocial","appealing","appendix","applaud","appraisal","appraise","appreciable"],"title":"A Winter in Antarctica","t":"Every year, a small team of scientists spends the long polar winter at a research station in the **Antarctic**. The station is resupplied **annually**, and for eight months the scientists have no contact with the outside world except through radio and satellite links. A tall **antenna** on the roof is their lifeline.\n\nLife there is **analogous** to life on a spaceship. Space agencies have even studied the station as an **analogue** of a mission to Mars. One psychologist used a simple **analogy**: \"Living in Antarctica is like living inside a small box that you cannot leave.\" People who are naturally **antisocial** rarely last long, because the team must cooperate closely.\n\nThe members have very different jobs. One is a weather **analyst** who sends daily reports to scientists around the world. Another uses **analytic** software to study ice samples, which record changes in the climate over thousands of years. Their work requires an **analytical** mind and great patience. A chemist tests new batteries in the extreme cold, carefully measuring how the **anode** reacts at minus sixty degrees.\n\nThe winter can bring unexpected dangers. Last year, a young engineer suffered a sudden pain in his side; his **appendix** had become inflamed. With no way to fly him out, the station doctor performed the operation herself. Everyone stood outside the room and began to **applaud** when she came out smiling.\n\nSmall comforts make life more **appealing**. The station has an **antique** piano, left by an expedition decades ago, and the team celebrates the station's **anniversary** with a special dinner. At the end of the season, each member writes an **appraisal** of the experience. An **anonymous** donor recently paid for a new greenhouse, and scientists will **appraise** its results next spring. Even a small supply of fresh vegetables makes an **appreciable** difference to morale."},"5":{"w":["appreciation","apt","arc","arch","arctic","array","arrogant","articulate","ascend","ascertain","ashore","aspiration","ass","assassinate","assault","assert","assertive","assessment","asset","assimilate"],"title":"Journey to the Arctic","t":"Last summer, a team of young researchers sailed north to the **Arctic** to study how melting ice affects local wildlife. Their ship carried an impressive **array** of instruments, from underwater cameras to drones. For the team leader, Dr. Sun, the trip was the realisation of a lifelong **aspiration**.\n\nWhen they finally went **ashore** on a small island, the landscape took their breath away. Ice cliffs rose above the sea, and the wind had carved one of them into a natural **arch**. The sun moved in a low **arc** across the sky and never set. Dr. Sun said that \"silent\" was the most **apt** word to describe the place.\n\nThe team's first task was to **ascertain** how much the ice had retreated since the previous survey. Two members had to **ascend** a steep glacier with ropes and ice axes. Their **assessment** was worrying: the ice had shrunk faster than expected. Local guides said polar bears were being forced to **assimilate** to new hunting grounds.\n\nThe expedition had difficult moments. One member, an **arrogant** young scientist, refused to follow the guide's advice and almost fell into a crevasse. Afterwards he admitted he had been a stubborn **ass**. Strong winds launched a fierce **assault** on their tents one night, and everyone stayed awake until dawn. To pass the time, someone read aloud from a historical novel about a plot to **assassinate** a king.\n\nDr. Sun is calm but **assertive**. She does not hesitate to **assert** her authority when safety is at stake. She is also **articulate**, explaining complex science in simple words. Back home, she gave talks about the trip, and audiences showed great **appreciation** for her photographs. \"Our planet's ice is a priceless **asset**,\" she told them. \"Once it is gone, we cannot buy it back.\""},"6":{"w":["assimilation","assurance","astonishment","astronomy","athletic","attachment","attendance","attendant","attorney","augment","authentic","automate","avail","availability","avert","axial","axle","backbone","bacon","bacterium"],"title":"Saving the Old Observatory","t":"On a hill above our town stands a small observatory built in the 1930s. For decades it was the home of the local **astronomy** club, but ten years ago the city announced plans to sell the land to a developer. Club members reacted with **astonishment**: few of them had imagined that the building could disappear.\n\nThe club's chairman, a retired engineer named Mr. Fu, became the **backbone** of the campaign to save it. He hired an **attorney** who agreed to work for free, and the two of them examined every document relating to the site. The attorney gave him **assurance** that the original owner's will had donated the land to the public, and the sale might therefore be illegal.\n\nMeanwhile, the club worked to **augment** its membership. It organised open nights, and **attendance** rose from a dozen people to over two hundred. The **assimilation** of so many newcomers was not always easy, but young, **athletic** members carried heavy equipment up the hill and brought fresh ideas. One student wrote software to **automate** the telescope's movements; another repaired the rusty **axle** of the rotating dome, so that it could once again turn smoothly around its **axial** support.\n\nVisitors were delighted to discover that the main telescope was **authentic**, made in Germany in 1931. A volunteer **attendant** guided guests up the narrow stairs and explained the history of each instrument. On cold winter mornings, the volunteers shared a simple breakfast of **bacon** and eggs before going home.\n\nThe campaign was not quick. Early appeals to the city council were to no **avail**, and the **availability** of public money remained uncertain. But in the end, the court ruled in the club's favour, and the sale was cancelled. \"We managed to **avert** a disaster,\" Mr. Fu says. Even a lab test that found a harmful **bacterium** in the building's old water tank did not reduce the members' **attachment** to the place; they simply replaced the pipes."},"7":{"w":["badge","baffle","bait","bald","ballet","ballot","bamboo","bandage","bandit","banker","bankrupt","bankruptcy","banquet","barge","barley","barometer","baron","barren","basement","batch"],"title":"The Baron's House","t":"On the edge of our city stands a grand house built in the 1890s by a wealthy **banker**. He was later given the title of **baron** for his services to the government, and he loved to hold an elaborate **banquet** in the great hall for hundreds of guests. Today the house is a museum, and visitors can explore every room, from the painted ballroom to the dark **basement** where wine was once stored.\n\nThe baron's fortune came from trade. Every week, a **barge** loaded with grain and **barley** travelled down the river to his warehouses. He kept a brass **barometer** in his study and checked it every morning, because storms could delay his boats. A display case shows a silver **badge** he wore at official ceremonies.\n\nHis success did not last. In 1921, after a series of bad investments, the bank went **bankrupt**. The **bankruptcy** shocked the city, and many ordinary families lost their savings. Historians remain **baffled|baffle** by his decisions; some believe he was tricked by dishonest partners, who used false promises as **bait**. Others note that **bandits|bandit** robbed several of his grain shipments in the countryside that year.\n\nAfter the collapse, the baron lived quietly in two rooms of the house. Photographs from that time show a thin, **bald** old man sitting in a garden that had become **barren**. When the city held a **ballot** on whether to buy the property, citizens voted overwhelmingly in favour.\n\nToday the museum is full of life. A local dance school performs **ballet** in the ballroom each summer, and craftsmen sell **bamboo** baskets in the courtyard. Each new **batch** of schoolchildren hears the guide tell the same story: on the day of the bankruptcy, the baron fell on the stairs, and a servant wrapped his injured hand in a simple **bandage**. \"Money can vanish in a day,\" the guide says. \"Kindness lasts longer.\""},"8":{"w":["bazaar","bead","bearing","beetle","beneficiary","benign","bestow","beware","bewilder","bias","biased","bid","biotech","bishop","bitterness","bizarre","blacksmith","bleach","bleak","blink"],"title":"The Charity Bazaar","t":"Every December, a church in the old part of our city holds a charity **bazaar** to raise money for the children's hospital. The **bishop** opens the event with a short speech, and then hundreds of visitors crowd between the stalls. The hospital is the sole **beneficiary**, and last year the bazaar raised more than two hundred thousand yuan.\n\nThe stalls offer an amazing variety of goods. Elderly women sell necklaces made of glass **beads|bead**, students sell homemade cakes, and a local **blacksmith** demonstrates his craft, hammering red-hot iron into hooks and candle holders. One stall sells jewellery made from the shells of a shiny green **beetle**, which many visitors find **bizarre**.\n\nThe highlight is an auction. Last year, a painting donated by a famous artist attracted a **bid** of fifty thousand yuan from a **biotech** company. The auctioneer, a calm man with a dignified **bearing**, did not even **blink** when the price kept rising.\n\nNot everything goes smoothly. One year, two stall owners argued over the best location, and the **bitterness** lasted for months. Another year, visitors complained that the judges of the cake competition were **biased** in favour of their friends. To avoid any **bias**, the organisers now use blind tasting.\n\nThe weather in December is often **bleak**, with grey skies and a cold wind, but nothing keeps people away. Volunteers clean the church floors with **bleach** before and after the event, and signs warn visitors to **beware** of pickpockets in the crowd. Newcomers are sometimes **bewildered|bewilder** by the noise and colour, but they soon relax. The atmosphere is warm and **benign**, and at the end of the day the bishop will **bestow** a small prize on the most popular stall."},"9":{"w":["blond","blouse","blunder","blunt","bluntly","blur","blush","boiler","bonus","bourgeois","boycott","brace","breakdown","bribe","bridegroom","bridle","brightness","broaden","browse","brutal"],"title":"A Wedding Full of Surprises","t":"When my cousin Wei got married last spring, everything that could go wrong did go wrong. The **bridegroom**, my cousin, arrived late because his car suffered a **breakdown** on the motorway. Then the hotel's **boiler** failed, and guests had to wash in cold water on the coldest morning of the year. \"We should have **braced|brace** ourselves for disaster,\" he joked later.\n\nThe bride, a quiet **blond** woman from Norway, handled it all with grace. She had chosen a simple white **blouse** and a long skirt instead of an expensive dress, which surprised some relatives. My grandmother, who dislikes anything **bourgeois**, declared it \"the most sensible wedding outfit\" she had ever seen.\n\nEarlier, my aunt had threatened to **boycott** the wedding because the couple had refused to hold it in her hometown. My father spoke to her **bluntly**: \"Be **blunt** with me if you like,\" he said, \"but don't punish the children.\" She came in the end, though she complained about the **brutal** cold all day.\n\nThe best man made a terrible **blunder** in his speech, calling the bride by the name of Wei's former girlfriend. The room went silent, and he began to **blush** deeply. Fortunately, the bride laughed, and the moment passed. The hotel manager, embarrassed by all the problems, gave the staff a **bonus** for working extra hours and refused a guest's offer of money to fix the heating faster. \"We don't take a **bribe**,\" he said with a smile.\n\nThe final surprise came in the evening, when a horse-drawn carriage arrived; the horse wore a **bridle** decorated with flowers. In the **brightness** of the lanterns, everything seemed to **blur** into a golden dream. Now, when I **browse** the wedding photos, I laugh at every disaster. The day helped to **broaden** my idea of what a perfect wedding is."},"10":{"w":["buddhism","buffalo","bug","bugle","bulletin","bump","bureaucracy","burglar","burial","burner","bushel","butt","buzz","by-product","bypass","calculus","calibration","cane","capability","capacitance"],"title":"The Biogas Engineer","t":"After graduating with a degree in engineering, my friend Lan returned to her hometown, a farming village in the south, to build a biogas plant. The village had plenty of animal waste from pigs and **buffalo**, and Lan wanted to turn it into clean fuel. The idea created a real **buzz** among the villagers.\n\nHer first challenge was **bureaucracy**. It took six months and dozens of forms to get permission to build. She found a way to **bypass** some delays by working with the village committee, which posted a **bulletin** explaining the project. A few people made her the **butt** of their jokes, calling her \"the girl who cooks with manure.\"\n\nThe technical work demanded every **capability** she had. She used **calculus** to model the flow of gas through the pipes, and careful **calibration** of the pressure sensors was essential. One sensor failed because of a **bug** in its software; another gave strange readings because of changes in the **capacitance** of its circuit in humid weather.\n\nThe plant opened after a year. Each kitchen received a new gas **burner**, and the slurry left behind became a valuable **by-product**: rich fertiliser that increased rice yields by several **bushels|bushel** per field. Farmers also used it on their sugar **cane**.\n\nVillage life continues around the plant. The road is full of every **bump** and hole, and trucks travel slowly. An old man who once played the **bugle** in the army still plays it at the village festival. Most families follow **Buddhism**, and the monks at the temple blessed the plant on its opening day; they also asked Lan to move one pipe away from an ancient **burial** ground. When a **burglar** tried to steal copper wire from the site, villagers caught him within an hour. \"Here,\" Lan says, \"everybody watches over everybody.\""},"11":{"w":["capacitor","cape","capsule","cartoon","cartridge","casualty","catalyst","catastrophe","category","cater","catholic","causal","caution","cavity","censor","centralize","ceramic","cereal","charcoal","charm"],"title":"The Night the Typhoon Came","t":"Our small fishing town sits on a rocky **cape** that points into the South China Sea. Typhoons are common here, but the storm that struck three years ago was a true **catastrophe**. It was classified in the highest **category**, with winds of over two hundred kilometres an hour.\n\nThe town had received warnings two days earlier, and the mayor urged extreme **caution**. Families were told to move to the old **Catholic** church on the hill, whose thick stone walls had survived storms for over a century. Volunteers carried bags of rice, boxes of **cereal** and sacks of **charcoal** for cooking, and a local restaurant agreed to **cater** for everyone in the shelter.\n\nThat night, the wind tore roofs from houses and flooded the harbour. Remarkably, there was only one **casualty**: a fisherman who broke his arm while securing his boat. Experts later explained that the low number of injuries had a clear **causal** link to the early evacuation.\n\nInside the church, people tried to stay calm. Children watched a **cartoon** on a laptop until its battery died, and an old radio stopped working when a **capacitor** burned out. A retired electrician repaired it with parts from a broken television. When the printer ran out of ink, someone found a spare **cartridge** so that volunteers could print lists of missing people.\n\nThe storm became a **catalyst** for change. The government decided to **centralize** emergency planning and built a new shelter with solar power. Some officials tried to **censor** reports criticising the slow repairs, but journalists published them anyway. The storm also exposed a large **cavity** beneath the old sea wall, which engineers have since filled.\n\nToday, a **ceramic** plaque on the church wall honours the volunteers, and pupils have buried a time **capsule** beside it, filled with letters about that night. The town has kept its old **charm**, but people no longer take the sea for granted."},"12":{"w":["charter","chasm","chatter","cherish","chestnut","chip","chord","chorus","chronic","chronicle","cigar","cigaret","circulation","circus","cite","clamp","clash","clatter","cleanliness","clearing"],"title":"Life with the Travelling Circus","t":"For six years, my uncle worked with a travelling **circus** that toured small towns across the north of the country. The company moved every week on a **charter** bus, followed by trucks carrying tents, animals and equipment. Whenever they arrived, they set up their big tent in a forest **clearing** or on an empty football field.\n\nHe loved the life, though it was never easy. The **chatter** of performers filled the camp from morning till night, and the **clatter** of hammers and metal poles began at dawn. The ringmaster smoked a fat **cigar** during rehearsals, while the acrobats rolled their own cigarettes; one old poster even advertised \"no **cigaret** smoking inside the tent.\" Despite the chaos, the manager insisted on strict **cleanliness**, and every animal cage was cleaned twice a day.\n\nNot everyone got along. A violent **clash** between two clowns once nearly ended the season, and the manager had to **clamp** down on fighting. Some performers suffered **chronic** back pain from years of hard training, and money was always a problem. Between the old performers and the young ones there seemed to be a **chasm** of different values.\n\nBut there were moments of pure magic. At the end of every show, the whole company sang a **chorus** together, and the band played a final **chord** that made the audience cheer. In autumn, children sold roasted **chestnuts|chestnut** outside the tent, and the smell of hot potato **chips|chip** filled the air.\n\nMy uncle kept a diary, a **chronicle** of every town they visited. A local newspaper with a wide **circulation** later published parts of it, and historians now **cite** it as a rare record of circus life. He still **cherishes|cherish** those years and says they taught him that joy is the best medicine."},"13":{"w":["client","climax","cling","clinic","clinical","clip","clockwise","closet","clown","cluster","clutch","coalition","coastal","coefficient","coffin","cognitive","coherent","coincide","coincidence","collaboration"],"title":"The Hospital Clown","t":"Every Tuesday, a man with a red nose and enormous shoes walks into the children's **clinic** at our city hospital. He is a professional **clown**, and his job is to make sick children laugh. Doctors say his visits have real **clinical** value: laughter reduces stress and can even help patients recover faster.\n\nHe knows this from experience. Ten years ago, he was seriously injured in a car crash on a **coastal** highway. He remembers how he would **clutch** the hand of a nurse during painful treatment and **cling** to the hope that he would walk again. \"I was nearly put in a **coffin**,\" he jokes. A hospital clown visited him every week, and he decided that if he recovered, he would do the same for others.\n\nHis work is carefully planned. He meets with doctors before each visit to learn about every young **client**, and he adapts his performance to each child's age and **cognitive** abilities. He carries a **closet** full of costumes and props, and he keeps funny photos in a **clip** on his belt. One favourite trick involves a toy clock whose hands run **clockwise**, then backwards, while he pretends to travel in time.\n\nThe programme is a **collaboration** between the hospital and a **coalition** of charities. Researchers have even measured its effects. They found a strong correlation **coefficient** between the number of visits and the children's mood scores, and they published a **coherent** report recommending clown visits in every children's hospital.\n\nThe **climax** of each visit comes when a **cluster** of children gathers around him for a magic show. By **coincidence**, his first day at the hospital happened to **coincide** with his own birthday, and the children made him a card. He has kept it ever since."},"14":{"w":["collide","colonial","colonist","combat","combustion","commence","commend","commentary","commodity","commonplace","commonsense","commonwealth","commute","compact","compatibility","compatible","compatriot","compensate","compensation","competence"],"title":"Commuting in a Former Colony","t":"Two years ago, my friend Xin moved to Singapore to work as an engineer. Like many young professionals there, she faces a daily **commute** of nearly an hour, first by bus and then by train. She uses the time to read the news or listen to the morning **commentary** on the radio.\n\nThe city is a fascinating mix of old and new. Its centre is full of **colonial** buildings from the nineteenth century, when British **colonist** families controlled the island's trade. Singapore is still a member of the **Commonwealth**, and English remains the main language of business. Xin has met many **compatriots|compatriot** from China, and they often share meals together at weekends.\n\nHer work involves improving public transport. She studies how buses and trains might **collide** in crowded junctions and designs systems to **combat** delays. Recently, her team tested a new electric bus that produces no **combustion** emissions. Its design is so **compact** that it can turn easily in narrow streets.\n\nShe has learned a lot about teamwork. **Compatibility** between team members matters as much as technical skill, she says. At first, she found it hard to work with a manager whose habits were not **compatible** with hers, but they gradually learned to respect each other. Her manager has since begun to **commend** her **competence** in public meetings.\n\nLife abroad also brings problems that are **commonplace** for migrants. Rent is high, and food is a costly **commodity**. When her flight home was cancelled last winter, the airline offered **compensation** of five hundred dollars, though it could not **compensate** for missing the Spring Festival. Her advice to newcomers is **commonsense**: be patient, ask questions and stay in touch with your family. A new project will **commence** next month, and she is excited to begin."},"15":{"w":["competitor","complement","completion","complexity","complication","compliment","comply","composer","composite","comprehend","compression","compulsory","concede","conceit","conceive","concentrated","conception","concession","concise","confer"],"title":"Writing a Symphony","t":"For three years, a young **composer** named Lu has been working on her first symphony. Last spring, she finally reached its **completion**, and the city orchestra agreed to perform it. \"I could hardly **comprehend** it,\" she says. \"It felt like a dream.\"\n\nThe idea for the symphony was **conceived|conceive** during a long train journey across western China. Her original **conception** was simple: four movements describing four seasons in the desert. As she wrote, however, the **complexity** of the work grew. She added parts for traditional instruments to **complement** the orchestra, creating a **composite** sound that mixes Eastern and Western traditions.\n\nThe process brought many a **complication**. Lu had to **comply** with strict rules set by the orchestra, which required that the piece last no more than forty minutes. She was forced to **concede** that some sections were too long, and she made a painful **concession** by cutting her favourite passage. \"Good music must be **concise**,\" her teacher told her. \"Every note must earn its place.\"\n\nShe also learned to avoid **conceit**. When an early recording received praise online, she was tempted to stop revising. But a friend warned her that a **competitor** in an international contest had written a similar piece, and she decided to work harder. She spent weeks in **concentrated** effort, sometimes practising for twelve hours a day. To send recordings to the orchestra, she used audio **compression**, though she always preferred live sound.\n\nOn the opening night, the audience stood and cheered. The music critic of a major newspaper paid her a rare **compliment**, calling the piece \"fresh and fearless.\" Next year, the music academy will **confer** an award on her. Lu now teaches a **compulsory** course on composition, and she tells her students that patience matters more than talent."},"16":{"w":["confidential","configuration","confirmation","conform","conformity","confront","confrontation","congest","conqueror","consecutive","consensus","consequent","consistently","console","consolidate","conspicuous","constituent","constitute","constrain","constraint"],"title":"The Battle over the Old Square","t":"In the centre of our city lies an old square dominated by a bronze statue of a medieval **conqueror** on horseback. For years, cars and buses have crowded the roads around it, and traffic has grown worse for five **consecutive** years. During rush hour, the streets become so **congested|congest** that a short trip can take forty minutes.\n\nLast year, the city council proposed a bold plan: close the square to cars and turn it into a pedestrian zone. A **confidential** report, later leaked to the press, showed that pollution in the area **consistently** exceeded national limits. The **consequent** public anger forced the council to act.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the idea. Shop owners feared they would lose customers, and several of them decided to **confront** the mayor at a public meeting. The **confrontation** was tense, but the mayor listened patiently. She tried to **console** worried business owners, promising financial support during the changes. \"Our **constituents|constituent** deserve clean air,\" she said, \"but we must also protect their jobs.\"\n\nReaching a **consensus** took months. Planners had to work within strict **constraints|constraint**, since a lack of money and space would **constrain** every design. Engineers tested several **configurations|configuration** of bus routes on a computer **console** before choosing the best one. The final plan had to **conform** to national safety rules, and some critics complained that the city was demanding too much **conformity** from local businesses.\n\nEventually, the council received **confirmation** that the national government would fund the project. The new square opened last month. Trees and benches now **constitute** most of the space, and a **conspicuous** fountain stands beside the statue. The mayor hopes to **consolidate** the plan's success by closing two more streets next year."},"17":{"w":["consul","consultancy","consultant","contaminate","contamination","contemplate","contemptuous","contend","context","continental","contradict","contradictory","contrive","contrived","controversial","controversy","convene","converge","converse","convict"],"title":"The Poisoned River","t":"Three years ago, farmers in a valley in the south noticed that their crops were dying and their fish were disappearing. Tests revealed that a chemical factory upstream had begun to **contaminate** the river. The **contamination** spread quickly, because three smaller streams **converge** just below the factory.\n\nThe case quickly became **controversial**. The factory's owners hired an international **consultancy** to defend them, and its **consultant** argued that the pollution came from natural sources. Local scientists **contended|contend** that this was nonsense; their data showed a clear connection with the factory's waste pipe. The two reports were completely **contradictory**, and each side accused the other of trying to **contrive** evidence.\n\nThe **controversy** attracted attention far beyond the valley. A foreign **consul**, whose country had invested in the factory, visited the area and met with farmers. Journalists noted the **contemptuous** tone of the factory managers, who refused to **converse** with villagers and dismissed their complaints as \"emotional.\" One manager's apology, written by a public relations firm, sounded so **contrived** that it made people even angrier.\n\nTo understand the case, it helps to consider the wider **context**. Across the region, rapid industrial growth had brought jobs but also serious environmental damage, a pattern seen in many **continental** economies. Officials had often ignored the problem.\n\nEventually, the provincial government decided to **convene** a special court. The judge reviewed the evidence carefully, and no witness was able to **contradict** the scientists' findings. The court **convicted|convict** two managers of breaking environmental laws and ordered the company to clean the river. Today the water is clear again. Many farmers now **contemplate** the future with cautious hope."},"18":{"w":["conviction","convincing","cooperative","corporate","corps","correctly","correlate","correlation","correspondence","corrode","corrosion","corrupt","cosmic","cosmos","costume","couch","counsel","counseling","counteract","counterpart"],"title":"From Office to Planetarium","t":"For fifteen years, Mr. He worked in a large **corporate** office, writing financial reports and attending endless meetings. He had a good salary, but he felt empty. In the evenings, he would lie on his **couch** and read books about the **cosmos**, dreaming of a different life.\n\nFive years ago, he made a decision. With a group of friends, he set up a **cooperative** that runs a small planetarium for schoolchildren. Every member owns a share of the business and has an equal vote. \"I had a deep **conviction** that science should be fun,\" he says. \"Children need to see the beauty of **cosmic** events with their own eyes.\"\n\nThe planetarium is in an old factory building, and renovating it was difficult. Years of rain had caused metal beams to **corrode**, and engineers had to treat the **corrosion** before installing the dome. Mr. He spent hours on **correspondence** with suppliers, and he sometimes discovered that his **counterpart** at a supplier had sent the wrong parts.\n\nThe shows are popular. Actors wear **costumes|costume** of famous astronomers, and a **corps** of volunteer students helps visitors find their seats. Teachers say that children who attend tend to perform better in science; one study found a strong **correlation** between visits and test scores, though it is hard to **correlate** the two **correctly** without more data.\n\nMr. He's life has not been free of trouble. A **corrupt** official once demanded money for a building permit, and he refused. He later sought legal **counsel** and received **counseling** to deal with the stress. \"The best way to **counteract** fear,\" he says, \"is to look up at the stars. They remind you how small our problems are.\" His argument is **convincing**: every year, more people leave the city lights to visit his dark, quiet dome."},"19":{"w":["courageous","courteous","courtesy","cozy","crab","cradle","crank","credential","crippling","crisp","criterion","crooked","cruelty","cruise","cuckoo","culminate","cumulative","curative","curb","curly"],"title":"The Café by the Harbour","t":"At the end of a **crooked** street near the old harbour, there is a small café run by a woman named Mrs. Lin. It is a **cozy** place with wooden tables, warm lights and a clock with a little **cuckoo** that sings every hour. Tourists from passing **cruise** ships often stop there, attracted by the smell of fresh bread.\n\nMrs. Lin is famous for two things: her **crisp** fried **crab** and her kindness. She is always **courteous** to customers, even rude ones, and she treats every guest with the same **courtesy**. Her husband, a cheerful man with **curly** grey hair, still uses an old coffee grinder with a hand **crank**.\n\nTheir story is more dramatic than most visitors realise. Ten years ago, Mrs. Lin suffered a **crippling** illness that kept her in bed for months. The **cumulative** cost of treatment almost forced the family to sell the café. She tried every medicine, including traditional herbs said to have **curative** power, and she slowly recovered. Her doctors called her **courageous**.\n\nDuring those months, she thought deeply about her life. She had once worked in a big hotel, where she saw a manager treat staff with shocking **cruelty**. She promised herself that if she ever ran a business, kindness would be its main **criterion** for success.\n\nToday, she hires young people who have no formal **credentials|credential** and trains them herself. She says the town where she grew up was the **cradle** of her values: honest, simple and generous. She works hard to **curb** waste, giving leftover food to the poor every evening. Her efforts **culminated|culminate** last year in a city award for community service, but she says the best reward is a full café on a rainy afternoon."},"20":{"w":["currency","currently","curriculum","curt","customary","cutter","cylinder","cynical","damn","datum","dazzle","deafen","dealer","dean","deceptive","decidedly","decimal","decisive","declaration","decompose"],"title":"The New Dean","t":"Last autumn, our engineering faculty welcomed a new **dean**, Professor Han. She had spent twenty years working in industry, and she arrived with **decidedly** different ideas from her predecessor. Many older teachers were **cynical**. \"New leaders always promise change,\" one said, \"and nothing ever happens.\"\n\nProfessor Han proved them wrong. Within a month, she made a **decisive** announcement: the faculty would redesign its entire **curriculum**. Students would spend less time memorising formulas and more time building real machines. \"A single **datum** is useless,\" she said, \"unless you know what to do with it.\" She issued a **declaration** of goals, which included opening a new workshop by the end of the year.\n\nThe workshop **currently** contains machines that **dazzle** first-year students: a laser **cutter**, 3D printers and an engine test room where a steel **cylinder** spins at enormous speed. The noise can **deafen** anyone who forgets to wear ear protection. A local car **dealer** donated two old engines, and students take them apart to see how every part works.\n\nNot every change was popular. Some teachers found her **curt** emails rude, and it was **customary** for staff to discuss decisions over long meetings, which she often cancelled. One professor muttered \"**damn** efficiency\" after a meeting lasted only ten minutes. Others warned that her plans were **deceptive**: they looked simple but required huge budgets in a foreign **currency** for imported equipment.\n\nBut results speak for themselves. In a chemistry project last term, students designed a system that uses bacteria to **decompose** plastic waste. In another, first-year students built a calculator that converts **decimal** numbers into binary code. Professor Han smiles when people praise her. \"I didn't do anything,\" she says. \"I just opened the doors.\""},"21":{"w":["decorative","decree","dedicate","deduct","deem","default","defendant","deferential","deficiency","deficient","deficit","deflect","deflection","deform","deformation","defy","degenerate","degradation","degrade","delicacy"],"title":"The Bridge on Trial","t":"Five years ago, a new bridge opened across the river in our city. It was praised for its elegant design and **decorative** lamps, and the mayor said it was **dedicated|dedicate** to the workers who built it. But within two years, engineers noticed something alarming: the main beam had begun to bend under heavy traffic.\n\nTests showed that the **deflection** of the beam was far greater than expected. Over time, the steel continued to **deform**, and cracks appeared in the concrete. Experts concluded that the **deformation** was caused by a serious **deficiency** in the materials. The steel used in the beam was **deficient** in strength, and rain had caused the concrete to **degrade** faster than normal.\n\nThe city took the construction company to court. The company, the **defendant** in the case, tried to **deflect** blame onto the designers. Its lawyers argued that the bridge had been used in ways that **defy** common sense, such as carrying trucks far heavier than allowed. But witnesses told a different story. One engineer, speaking in a quiet and **deferential** tone, admitted that managers had ordered workers to use cheaper steel to reduce costs.\n\nThe company was already in financial trouble. It had a large budget **deficit** and had gone into **default** on several bank loans. Some observers argued that its culture had begun to **degenerate** long before the bridge was built, as managers cared more about profits than safety.\n\nAfter months of hearings, the judge issued a **decree**: the company must pay for repairs, and the city would **deduct** the cost from any money it still owed. The judge **deemed|deem** the managers' behaviour a serious breach of public trust. The case showed how the slow **degradation** of standards can endanger lives. Handling public anger was a matter of great **delicacy** for the city, but the repaired bridge reopened last month, stronger than before."},"22":{"w":["delusion","demography","denial","denote","denounce","dentist","dependant","depict","deploy","deportation","deposition","deprive","derivation","descendant","descent","designate","desolate","despatch","desperately","dessert"],"title":"Tracing a Family's Roots","t":"Last year, my cousin decided to study our family history. She is a **descendant** of a farming family from Shandong, and she wanted to understand the **derivation** of our unusual surname. Her research took her through dusty archives, old letters and **desolate** villages that most people have forgotten.\n\nShe began with **demography**. Population records showed that our ancestors moved north in the 1890s, part of a mass migration caused by famine. Many families were **deprived|deprive** of their land and had to start again with nothing. Some records **denote** the number of each family's **dependants|dependant**, and she found that one ancestor had supported eleven relatives on a small farm.\n\nThe most moving discoveries were letters. One great-grandfather, a **dentist** in a small town, wrote to his brother in **desperately** sad words after his wife died. Another letter, sent by military **despatch** in 1938, described how soldiers were **deployed|deploy** near the village during the war. A third **depicted|depict** the family's **descent** into poverty after a flood destroyed their house. The flood also left a thick **deposition** of mud across the fields, which took years to clear.\n\nNot every relative welcomed the project. Some lived in **denial** about painful parts of our past, and one uncle insisted that our family had been wealthy landowners. \"That's a **delusion**,\" my cousin said gently. She also discovered that an ancestor had once publicly **denounced|denounce** a corrupt official, and that the official had arranged the **deportation** of his whole family to a distant province.\n\nWhen the research was complete, she **designated|designate** a room in her flat as a family archive. At our New Year dinner, she shared what she had learned. Over **dessert**, the room fell silent as she read the old letters aloud."},"23":{"w":["destine","destiny","detach","detain","detective","detector","deteriorate","deviate","deviation","devotion","devour","diagnose","diagnostic","dictator","diesel","diffuse","dignity","dilemma","diligence","dilute"],"title":"The Detective Writer","t":"Ms. Wei writes **detective** novels, and readers **devour** them as soon as they appear. Her hero, an elderly policewoman, solves crimes with patience, **diligence** and a sharp eye for detail. Ms. Wei says her character was **destined|destine** to be old: \"Young detectives run; old ones think.\"\n\nHer latest book is set in a fictional country ruled by a **dictator**. The heroine is **detained|detain** at the border while investigating a murder and must decide whether to cooperate with the secret police or protect a witness. \"Every good mystery contains a moral **dilemma**,\" the author explains. \"The question is not only who did it, but what is right.\"\n\nMs. Wei researches every detail. For her new book, she visited a hospital to learn how doctors **diagnose** poisoning and spent days studying **diagnostic** tests. She also learned how a metal **detector** works and how a small **diesel** engine can be modified to start a fire. Her **devotion** to accuracy is famous among her readers.\n\nShe admits that writing is a lonely job. During long periods of work, she must **detach** herself from family life, and she has noticed that her health can **deteriorate** if she works too late. She tries to follow a fixed routine and never **deviate** from it; even a small **deviation**, she says, can destroy her concentration.\n\nHer books also explore deeper themes. In the latest one, the heroine refuses to lose her **dignity**, even in prison. Ms. Wei believes that crime fiction can **diffuse** important ideas about justice to a wide audience without lecturing them. \"If you **dilute** the story with too many messages, readers stop reading,\" she warns. \"But if you trust them, they understand.\" She does not believe in **destiny**; she believes in hard work."},"24":{"w":["diminish","dine","dioxide","diploma","diplomatic","directory","disagreement","disappearance","disapproval","disastrous","discern","discontented","discourse","discrepancy","discrete","disgrace","disillusion","disillusioned","dismay","disobedience"],"title":"The Diplomat's Daughter","t":"My friend Ling grew up as the daughter of a **diplomatic** officer, moving to a new country every three years. Her childhood was exciting, but it was not easy. Every move meant new schools, new languages and new friends, and she often felt **discontented** and lonely.\n\nHer father dealt with serious international issues. At one conference on climate change, he spent days negotiating limits on carbon **dioxide** emissions. The talks nearly collapsed when a **disagreement** broke out over the figures; there was a large **discrepancy** between two countries' data. Her father had to **discern** which numbers were accurate before the talks could continue.\n\nAt home, Ling sometimes rebelled. As a teenager in Paris, she skipped school for a week, and her **disobedience** caused her parents great **dismay**. Their **disapproval** was clear, but they did not shout. Instead, they invited her to **dine** with them at a quiet restaurant and asked her to explain her feelings. \"I realised they were also tired of moving,\" she remembers.\n\nAs she grew older, Ling became **disillusioned** with the glamorous image of diplomatic life. She saw how public **discourse** about international relations often ignored ordinary families. One case left a deep impression: the sudden **disappearance** of a journalist her father knew, which ended in tragedy. The incident was **disastrous** for relations between two countries, and her father called it a **disgrace**.\n\nToday Ling works as a teacher. She earned her **diploma** in education and keeps a **directory** of former classmates scattered across five continents. Her experience has not made her bitter; it did **disillusion** her about power, but it did not **diminish** her belief in people. She tells her students that the world is not a set of **discrete** nations but one connected community."},"25":{"w":["disorient","dispatch","dispense","disperse","displace","displacement","disposition","disproportionate","disregard","disrupt","disruptive","dissatisfaction","disservice","dissipate","distil","distinctive","distinctly","distort","distortion","distract"],"title":"The Open-Plan Office","t":"When our company moved to a new building last year, managers introduced an open-plan office. The walls between departments disappeared, and two hundred employees now share one large room. The idea was to **disperse** information more quickly and to encourage teamwork, but the result was **distinctly** mixed.\n\nFrom the first week, there was clear **dissatisfaction**. Phones rang constantly, colleagues chatted loudly, and people found it hard not to **distract** one another. For staff who need to concentrate, the noise was **disruptive**. Even the sound from the coffee machine, which can **dispense** thirty cups an hour, seemed to **disrupt** meetings.\n\nThe change also affected different groups in a **disproportionate** way. Engineers and writers, who need quiet, suffered most. The staff in the **dispatch** department, who send hundreds of parcels every day, found that the noise **distorted|distort** their phone calls. Some employees felt the new layout had been designed with complete **disregard** for their needs. One manager admitted that the old office's **distinctive** character had been lost.\n\nNew employees sometimes felt **disoriented|disorient**. The room was so large that people got lost looking for their teams. Older staff complained of the **displacement** of familiar routines, as if they had been **displaced|displace** from their own homes. A survey showed that the excitement of the new building quickly began to **dissipate**.\n\nFinally, the company hired a consultant, who tried to **distil** the problem into one sentence: \"You have done your staff a **disservice** by ignoring how they actually work.\" He warned that complaints should not be treated as a **distortion** of the truth. The company has since built quiet rooms and allowed flexible working hours. Our manager, a person of calm **disposition**, says the lesson is simple: listen before you build."},"26":{"w":["disturbance","diverge","diversify","diversion","divert","divine","dizzy","doctrine","documentary","domain","dome","dominant","donate","doom","dormant","dove","downturn","drainage","drastic","drawback"],"title":"A Town Reinvents Itself","t":"For most of the twentieth century, our town depended on a single coal mine. When the mine closed in 2010, many people believed the town was **doomed|doom**. The economic **downturn** was severe: unemployment doubled, young people left, and shops closed one after another.\n\nThe local government knew that **drastic** action was needed. Its plan was to **diversify** the economy by developing tourism, farming and small industries. The town's most famous building, an old temple with a golden **dome**, became the centre of the new strategy. Its monks agreed to open the temple to visitors, and a television company made a **documentary** about its history and religious **doctrine**.\n\nThe temple stands on a hill above a **dormant** volcano, and legend says the site has **divine** protection. Every morning, hundreds of white **doves|dove** circle the dome. Visitors who climb the narrow tower often feel **dizzy**, but the view across the valley is worth it.\n\nThe plan had its **drawbacks|drawback**. Building new roads caused noise and **disturbance** for residents, and engineers had to **divert** a stream to make room for a car park. The **diversion** of the water damaged the old **drainage** system, and several streets flooded during the summer rains. Opinions began to **diverge** sharply: some residents supported tourism, while others feared it would destroy the town's quiet character.\n\nToday, the results are encouraging. Tourism has become the **dominant** industry, and a local company has registered an internet **domain** to sell handmade crafts online. Former miners now work as guides, and wealthy visitors often **donate** money to repair the temple. \"We lost the mine,\" the mayor says, \"but we found a new future.\""},"27":{"w":["dreadful","dubious","duke","dully","duplicate","dwarf","dwell","dynamical","dynamo","earnings","earthenware","eastward","eccentric","eclipse","ecology","economically","ecosystem","edible","edit","ego"],"title":"The Eccentric Duke","t":"Two hundred years ago, a wealthy English **duke** built a grand house on a hill and surrounded it with a huge private forest. He was famously **eccentric**: he slept during the day, worked at night and refused to allow anyone to cut down a single tree. Neighbours considered his ideas **dubious**, but today his forest is one of the richest **ecosystems|ecosystem** in the country.\n\nThe duke was passionate about nature long before **ecology** became a science. He kept detailed notebooks recording the plants and animals that **dwell** in the forest, and he identified hundreds of **edible** mushrooms and berries. He also built a small **dynamo** powered by a stream to light his study, one of the first in the region, and wrote papers on the **dynamical** behaviour of water.\n\nHis personal life was less happy. He had a large **ego** and argued constantly with his family. His son described him as a **dreadful** father who spoke **dully** at dinner and thought only of his work. When the duke died, his son tried to sell the forest, but the will forbade it.\n\nToday, the estate belongs to a charity. Its **earnings** come from visitors, who walk along paths that run **eastward** through ancient oaks so tall that they **dwarf** the house. A small museum displays the duke's notebooks and his collection of **earthenware** pots. Volunteers are working to **duplicate** and **edit** his writings so that they can be published online.\n\nThe estate is now run **economically** and sustainably. Its most popular event takes place during a solar **eclipse**, when visitors gather on the lawn to watch the sky darken. \"The duke was strange,\" the director says, \"but he saw what others missed: that nature is worth more than money.\""},"28":{"w":["eject","elapse","electrician","electrode","elegant","elemental","elevate","elevation","elicit","eligible","elite","elliptical","eloquence","eloquent","embark","embassy","embody","embroidery","emigrate","emission"],"title":"Stitches Across the Sea","t":"My great-aunt Mei was born in a mountain village at an **elevation** of almost two thousand metres. As a girl, she learned the traditional **embroidery** of her region, covering cloth with **elegant** patterns of flowers and birds. Her work seemed to **embody** the spirit of the mountains: patient, careful and quietly beautiful.\n\nIn 1985, she decided to **emigrate** to Canada. She had to visit the **embassy** in Beijing several times before she became **eligible** for a visa, and when she finally **embarked|embark** on the long flight, she carried only one suitcase and a box of silk threads. Years later, she remembered how slowly the hours seemed to **elapse** on that journey.\n\nLife in Canada was hard at first. Her husband worked as an **electrician**, and she sewed clothes in a factory. She was often lonely, but her embroidery kept her connected to home. A local museum eventually noticed her work and invited her to give classes. Her lessons were simple, but she spoke with natural **eloquence**, and her students were moved by her stories. She once said that the best embroidery is like an **eloquent** silence.\n\nHer designs became famous. One, showing an **elliptical** moon over the mountains, was bought by an **elite** art collection in Toronto. Critics praised its **elemental** power: earth, water and sky seemed to live in every stitch. The museum's director said her work helped to **elevate** folk art to the level of fine art and **elicit** respect for immigrant cultures.\n\nMei is now ninety. She jokes about modern technology, especially machines that **eject** paper for no reason, and she worries about factory **emissions|emission** polluting the air. Her husband's old toolbox, full of wires and an **electrode** from a welding kit, still sits beside her sewing table."},"29":{"w":["empirical","enchant","enclosure","endanger","endeavor","endow","endurance","energetic","energize","engagement","enlighten","enrich","ensue","entail","enterprise","entity","entreat","entrepreneur","envious","environmental"],"title":"The Bird Farm","t":"Six years ago, a young **entrepreneur** named Zhao left a high-paying job in finance to start an unusual **enterprise**: a farm that protects rare birds. Friends thought he was mad, and some were secretly **envious** of his courage. \"I wanted my work to have meaning,\" he says.\n\nThe project required great **endurance**. Building the farm would **entail** years of work and most of his savings. He began by creating a large **enclosure** in a wetland area, where he planted native trees and grasses. Rapid development nearby threatened to **endanger** several species, and he hoped the farm would give them a safe home.\n\nZhao approaches conservation with an **empirical** method. He keeps careful records of bird numbers, nesting habits and food sources. His data have helped scientists **enlighten** the public about the importance of wetlands, and a university now treats the farm as an independent research **entity**.\n\nThe farm also tries to **enrich** the lives of local people. It employs twenty villagers and runs programmes to encourage community **engagement**. Children visit every week, and the sight of hundreds of cranes taking off together seems to **enchant** them. An **energetic** volunteer leads tours, and her enthusiasm can **energize** even the most tired visitors.\n\nSuccess did not come easily. When the farm was flooded in its second year, Zhao knew that months of difficulty would **ensue**. Zhao **entreated|entreat** local officials to help, and they eventually agreed to build better drainage. A wealthy businessman later offered to **endow** a research fund in his name. Zhao says every **endeavor** to protect nature is worthwhile. \"**Environmental** work is slow,\" he says, \"but the birds remind me why it matters.\""},"30":{"w":["envisage","epidemic","epoch","equate","equator","equilibrium","eradicate","erosion","errand","erroneous","erupt","eruption","escort","essence","eternal","ethical","ethics","ethnic","evaluation","evaporation"],"title":"The Island Doctor","t":"Dr. Okafor works at a small hospital on an island just north of the **equator**. The climate is hot and humid all year, and rapid **evaporation** means that water is often scarce in the dry season. Above the island rises an active volcano, which last saw a major **eruption** in 1998.\n\nWhen Dr. Okafor arrived ten years ago, the island was suffering from a malaria **epidemic**. She set out to **eradicate** the disease, a goal that many experts considered impossible. Her team handed out bed nets, drained standing water and trained local volunteers. \"At first, people **equated|equate** malaria with fate,\" she explains. \"They thought it was an **eternal** part of life.\"\n\nHer work also has an **ethical** side. Many patients come from **ethnic** minorities with their own healing traditions, and she believes doctors must respect them. Medical **ethics**, she says, require honesty and humility. When she discovered that an earlier health report contained **erroneous** data that hid the real number of deaths, she insisted on publishing the correct figures.\n\nIsland life is never dull. Children run **errands|errand** for the hospital, carrying messages and medicine between villages. When the volcano began to rumble last year, the government feared it might **erupt** again, and soldiers had to **escort** patients to a safer town. Heavy rains also cause soil **erosion** on the hillsides, cutting off roads for days.\n\nA recent **evaluation** showed that malaria cases have fallen by ninety percent. Dr. Okafor **envisages|envisage** a future in which the disease disappears completely. \"Health is a delicate **equilibrium** between people and nature,\" she says. \"The **essence** of medicine is not machines but trust.\" Some islanders say her arrival marked the beginning of a new **epoch**."},"31":{"w":["evenly","everlasting","evoke","evolutionary","excel","exceptional","excessively","exclamation","excrete","execution","exemplify","exempt","exile","exotic","expedition","experimentally","experimentation","expertise","expire","explicit"],"title":"In Search of Rare Plants","t":"Last spring, a team of botanists set off on an **expedition** to the rainforests of southern Yunnan. Their goal was to find a rare orchid that had not been seen for forty years. The leader, Professor Bao, is a scientist of **exceptional** experience whose **expertise** in tropical plants is recognised worldwide.\n\nThe forest was full of **exotic** life. Giant butterflies floated between the trees, and strange flowers seemed to **evoke** a world from millions of years ago. Professor Bao explained that the region is a living museum of **evolutionary** history. One plant they found **exemplifies|exemplify** this: a meat-eating vine that traps insects and then **excretes|excrete** chemicals to digest them.\n\nThe trip required careful planning and precise **execution**. The team's research permits would **expire** after thirty days, and the rules were **explicit**: no plants could be removed without permission. As a university research group, they were **exempt** from the usual park fees, but they had to submit daily reports.\n\nThe work was exhausting. Heavy rain fell almost every afternoon, and the humidity was **excessively** high. The team divided tasks **evenly**, and each member had a specific role. One young researcher seemed to **excel** at climbing trees, while another was skilled at photography.\n\nOn the twenty-sixth day, they finally found the orchid growing on a cliff. Professor Bao gave an **exclamation** of joy that echoed through the valley. Back in the laboratory, the team began careful **experimentation** to understand how the plant survives, testing **experimentally** how it responds to light and water. The professor's own story is moving: as a young scientist, he spent years in **exile** during a time of political trouble. \"Nature gave me hope,\" he says. \"Its beauty feels **everlasting**.\""},"32":{"w":["exploitation","exploration","exposition","exquisite","extinction","extinguish","extract","extraction","extracurricular","extraordinarily","extravagant","fable","fabricate","fabrication","facet","facilitate","faction","fake","fantastic","fascinate"],"title":"The Hall of Lost Animals","t":"Last month, our city museum opened an **exposition** called \"The Hall of Lost Animals.\" It tells the story of species that have disappeared because of human activity, from the dodo to the Yangtze river dolphin. The exhibition is **extraordinarily** popular, attracting thousands of visitors every weekend.\n\nEach room explores a different **facet** of the problem. One shows how the **exploitation** of forests and oceans has led to **extinction**. Another describes the age of **exploration**, when European sailors hunted birds and animals on remote islands. A beautiful glass case contains **exquisite** models of extinct birds, made by artists using real feathers donated by zoos.\n\nThe museum also displays a **fable** written by a children's author about the last dodo. Children love it, and the museum's **extracurricular** science club uses it to start discussions about protecting nature. Teachers say the exhibition helps to **facilitate** learning in a way that textbooks cannot.\n\nNot all the stories are sad. One room explains how scientists **extract** DNA from ancient bones, hoping one day to bring some species back. However, the process of **extraction** is controversial. A few years ago, a researcher was found to **fabricate** results, and his **fabrication** damaged public trust in the field. Today scientists are divided into two **factions|faction**: those who support \"de-extinction\" and those who think it is an **extravagant** waste of money.\n\nThe museum is careful to show only genuine specimens. A curator explained that one object donated as a rare bird egg turned out to be a **fake**. Another exhibit recalls a fire in a national park that took weeks to **extinguish**. The exhibition continues to **fascinate** visitors of all ages. One boy summed it up perfectly: \"It's **fantastic** and sad at the same time.\""},"33":{"w":["fascinating","fastener","faultless","fearless","fell","fellowship","feminine","ferrous","ferry","fiber","fighter","filament","filth","finely","finite","fireplace","firework","firmness","first-rate","fission"],"title":"The Fearless Physicist","t":"My grandmother was one of the first women in China to study nuclear physics. In the 1960s, when few women entered science, she won a research **fellowship** at a top university. People said physics was not a **feminine** subject, but she ignored them. Her colleagues described her as **fearless** and her work as **first-rate**.\n\nHer research focused on nuclear **fission**, the process in which atoms split and release energy. She also studied **ferrous** metals used in reactors and tested how a thin **filament** of tungsten behaves under extreme heat. Her laboratory notes, written **finely** in tiny characters, are almost **faultless**; she rarely made mistakes.\n\nShe grew up on an island, and she still loves to tell stories about her childhood. Every morning, she took a small **ferry** to school, and in winter, her father would **fell** trees for firewood. She remembers how the family gathered around the **fireplace** at night, and how fishermen set off **fireworks|firework** at the Spring Festival.\n\nHer life was not always easy. During difficult political years, she was sent to work in a factory, where conditions were terrible. She spent months cleaning **filth** from machines and sorting metal **fasteners|fastener** by hand. But she never lost her **firmness** of purpose. \"Time is **finite**,\" she said, \"so I studied at night.\"\n\nLater, she returned to research and helped design safety systems for power stations. She also worked on new materials, including a strong carbon **fiber** that could replace steel. Her students called her a **fighter**, because she refused to give up. Today, at eighty-five, she still reads science journals every day. \"Physics is **fascinating**,\" she says. \"Every answer leads to a new question.\""},"34":{"w":["fitness","fitting","fixture","flake","flank","flannel","flap","flask","flatten","flatter","flaw","fling","fluctuate","fluctuation","fluorescent","flutter","flux","foam","foil","follower"],"title":"The Fitness Star","t":"Two years ago, my cousin Jie was an overweight office worker who rarely exercised. Today, he runs a small gym and has more than half a million **followers|follower** online. His story shows how quickly lives can change, though it is not as simple as his videos suggest.\n\nHis gym is in an old warehouse, and two tall buildings **flank** its entrance. Inside, **fluorescent** lights shine on rows of machines, and the floor is covered with thick **foam** mats. Every light **fixture** was installed by Jie himself. There is also a small **fitting** room where customers can try on sports clothes.\n\nJie's online videos focus on **fitness** for ordinary people. He wears a simple **flannel** shirt and keeps a **flask** of green tea beside him. He refuses to **flatter** his viewers with false promises. \"Your weight will **fluctuate**,\" he tells them, \"and every **fluctuation** is normal.\" He warns that the industry is in constant **flux**, with new trends appearing every month.\n\nNot everything about his business is perfect. He admits that his early training plans had a serious **flaw**: they were too hard for beginners. Some viewers injured themselves trying to **fling** heavy weights. He now teaches gentle exercises, such as standing with arms out and slowly letting them **flap** like wings.\n\nHis lifestyle is simple. He eats oat **flakes|flake** for breakfast and wraps his lunch in **foil** to take to work. He still feels his heart **flutter** before filming a new video. \"The hardest thing is not exercise,\" he says. \"It's learning to **flatten** your fears. Once you do, everything else is easy.\""},"35":{"w":["footpath","fore","foresee","forge","formerly","formidable","formulate","formulation","forsake","fort","forthcoming","fortress","forum","fossil","foster","foul","fowl","fracture","fragile","fragrant"],"title":"The Fort on the Hill","t":"On a hill above my hometown stands an old stone **fort**, built in the sixteenth century to defend the coast against pirates. It was **formerly** an important military base, but today it is a peaceful park. A narrow **footpath** winds up the hill through pine trees and **fragrant** wild flowers.\n\nThe **fortress** was once a **formidable** sight. Its walls are ten metres high, and its gates were made of iron that local blacksmiths would **forge** by hand. Historians say the officers who designed it were able to **foresee** almost every kind of attack. Their strategy, which they had to **formulate** without modern maps, was remarkably effective.\n\nRecently, workers repairing the walls made an exciting discovery: a **fossil** of an ancient fish embedded in one of the stones. Scientists believe the rock came from a nearby quarry that was once under the sea. A **forthcoming** exhibition will display the fossil, and the town has organised a public **forum** to discuss how best to protect the site.\n\nThe fort has a darker side. During one long siege, the soldiers inside ran out of food and had to eat their horses and every **fowl** they kept. Records describe the **foul** smell of disease and the many soldiers who suffered a **fracture** from falling rocks. Some chose to **forsake** their posts and flee.\n\nToday, the fort is **fragile**. Rain and wind are slowly destroying its walls, and engineers are testing a new **formulation** of mortar to repair them. Local schools use the site to **foster** students' interest in history. The town's leaders hope to bring the fort's story to the **fore** of the region's tourism, so that visitors can understand how much the past still shapes the present."},"36":{"w":["frail","fraud","freshen","fret","fright","frightful","fringe","fro","frock","fulfill","funding","fury","fuse","fuss","galaxy","gamble","gangster","gaol","garlic","garment"],"title":"The Last Star of Galaxy Studios","t":"In the 1930s, a film company called **Galaxy** Studios made dozens of popular movies in Shanghai. Most of them were crime dramas about a **gangster** who would **gamble** away his fortune, cheat his friends and end up in **gaol**. Today, the old studio is a museum, and its most famous actress, now ninety-four, still visits every year.\n\nShe is **frail** now and walks with a stick, but her eyes are bright. When she arrived last spring, the staff made a great **fuss**, bringing flowers and tea. She laughed and told them not to **fret**. \"I'm old, not broken,\" she said.\n\nThe museum's collection of costumes is remarkable. Visitors can see a silk **frock** she wore in her first film, decorated with a long **fringe** of beads, as well as hundreds of other **garments|garment** from the period. One display explains how studio electricians often blew a **fuse** when too many lights were used at once.\n\nShe told stories about her early career. Once, during filming, a fire broke out on the set, and she remembers the **fright** of seeing flames climb the curtains. The director's **fury** afterwards was **frightful**; he shouted for an hour. She also recalled a **fraud** case in which a producer stole the studio's money and fled abroad.\n\nHer advice to young actors is simple: work hard and **fulfill** your promises. She also has a strange habit: before every performance, she eats a clove of raw **garlic** for luck, then uses mint to **freshen** her breath. As she walks to and **fro** among the displays, visitors follow her with their phones. The museum hopes to receive government **funding** to record her memories before it is too late."},"37":{"w":["gathering","generalization","generalize","generosity","genetics","geographical","geometrical","gigantic","ginger","glamour","gleam","glider","glorify","glossary","gnaw","goddess","gorgeous","gorilla","gossip","grab"],"title":"Among the Mountain Gorillas","t":"Dr. Amani has spent fifteen years studying mountain **gorillas|gorilla** in the forests of central Africa. Her research combines field observation with **genetics**, comparing DNA samples from different groups. She wants to understand how **geographical** barriers, such as rivers and mountains, separate populations.\n\nHer days are long and hard. She hikes for hours through thick forest, following signs left by the animals: broken branches, footprints and plants the gorillas **gnaw** on. When she finally finds a group, she sits quietly for hours. \"They are **gigantic** but gentle,\" she says. \"A large male can weigh two hundred kilograms, yet he plays with babies like a grandfather.\"\n\nShe warns against easy **generalization**. \"People love to **generalize** about animals,\" she explains, \"but every gorilla has its own personality.\" One young female, with **ginger**-coloured hair, is famously curious and often tries to **grab** Dr. Amani's notebook.\n\nResearch has none of the **glamour** shown in films. Dr. Amani lives in a small hut and often eats only beans and rice. But she says the forest is **gorgeous** at dawn, when mist rises and light begins to **gleam** on the leaves. Local people call one mountain the home of a forest **goddess**, and they treat it with deep respect.\n\nShe worries that documentaries **glorify** scientists while ignoring local rangers, whose **generosity** and courage make research possible. At village **gatherings|gathering**, she thanks them publicly. She has also written a simple **glossary** of scientific terms in the local language. Recently, her team began using a small **glider** drone to map the forest, flying in **geometrical** patterns to cover every area. \"We still have much to learn,\" she says, smiling at the **gossip** that she prefers gorillas to people. \"Maybe it's true.\""},"38":{"w":["gradient","gramophone","granite","graphite","grasshopper","grassy","gravel","graze","grease","greed","grief","grim","grin","groove","grope","grove","growl","grumble","grunt","guilt"],"title":"The Quarry Village","t":"My grandfather grew up in a village built around a **granite** quarry in the hills of Fujian. For two hundred years, almost every family there worked with stone. Men cut huge blocks from the hillside, and women sorted **gravel** and loaded it onto carts that rolled down the steep **gradient** to the river.\n\nLife was hard and often **grim**. The work was dangerous, and accidents were common. My grandfather remembers the **grief** of the whole village when three workers died in a rockfall. The quarry owner, a man known for his **greed**, refused to pay compensation, and many workers felt anger mixed with **guilt** that they had not protested sooner.\n\nYet there were happy memories too. In the evenings, children played in a **grassy** field beside a **grove** of pine trees, catching **grasshoppers|grasshopper** and watching goats **graze**. The village had one **gramophone**, and on summer nights its owner played old records while people danced. My grandfather loved the scratch of the needle as it moved along the record's **groove**.\n\nHe also remembers his first job, at the age of twelve. He had to **grease** the wheels of the carts with oil and clean the stone dust from the machines. Sometimes the lights failed, and he had to **grope** his way through the dark tunnels. An old worker taught him to draw with a pencil of **graphite**, and he discovered a talent for art.\n\nWorkers in the village would often **grumble** about wages, and the foreman would **growl** at anyone who stopped to rest. But my grandfather says the men rarely complained in front of children. When they finished a long day, they would **grunt** with exhaustion and then **grin** at each other. \"We were poor,\" he says, \"but we were never alone.\""},"39":{"w":["guitar","gust","gutter","hail","halve","ham","hamper","handbook","handicap","handout","harbor","hardy","harmonious","harp","hassle","haughty","haul","haunt","hazardous","headlong"],"title":"The Musician on the Pier","t":"Every evening, a man named Old Lu plays music on the pier of our city's **harbor**. He carries a battered **guitar** and a small folding **harp**, and he sets up beside the fish market as the sun goes down. Tourists stop to listen, and fishermen pause as they **haul** their nets onto the shore.\n\nOld Lu has a physical **handicap**: he lost his left leg in an accident thirty years ago. But it has never seemed to **hamper** his music. \"Life gave me a hard road,\" he says, \"so I made it **harmonious**.\" He learned to play from a **handbook** he found in a second-hand shop, practising every day for years.\n\nHis life is not easy. He sleeps in a small room near the fish market and depends on the money people drop into his hat. On bad nights, he earns almost nothing, and he must **halve** his meals to save money. Some passers-by are **haughty** and treat him like a beggar, offering a **handout** of coins without listening. He doesn't mind. \"Music is for everyone,\" he says.\n\nThe weather by the sea can be **hazardous**. Strong **gusts|gust** of wind blow across the pier, and in winter **hail** sometimes falls without warning. Once, a storm sent him running **headlong** for shelter, and his guitar fell into a **gutter** full of water. A local carpenter repaired it for free.\n\nOld Lu is a **hardy** man who rarely complains, though dealing with officials who demand permits is a constant **hassle**. In the evenings, a café owner brings him a **ham** sandwich and a cup of tea. His songs **haunt** those who hear them; long after they leave the pier, many visitors say they can still hear the music in their heads."},"40":{"w":["hearth","hearty","heater","heave","heighten","hemisphere","henceforth","herald","herb","heritage","hierarchy","hike","hinder","hindrance","hinge","hip","hiss","historian","historic","hitherto"],"title":"The House by the Mountain Pass","t":"On an old mountain road in Shanxi stands a stone farmhouse that is more than four hundred years old. It was recently declared a **historic** building, part of the region's cultural **heritage**. A local **historian**, Mr. Duan, has spent years studying it, and he says it tells the story of ordinary life in a way that palaces cannot.\n\nThe centre of the house is the **hearth**, where families cooked, ate and kept warm. Today, a modern **heater** stands in the corner, but visitors still gather around the old fireplace. Mr. Duan says that, **hitherto**, historians had paid little attention to such buildings. \"We studied emperors, not farmers,\" he explains.\n\nThe house reveals the strict **hierarchy** of traditional families. The oldest man slept in the warmest room, while young wives lived in small rooms near the kitchen. Even the doors show social rank: the main gate turns on a heavy iron **hinge**, while servants used a simple wooden door at the back.\n\nRestoring the house has been difficult. Heavy rain and a lack of money continue to **hinder** progress, and the narrow road is a major **hindrance** to delivering materials. Workers must **heave** stones up the slope by hand, and one older volunteer injured his **hip**.\n\nVisitors who **hike** to the house are rewarded with a **hearty** meal of noodles and wild **herbs|herb**. On cold nights, the wind seems to **hiss** through the pass, which only seems to **heighten** the sense of history. Mr. Duan believes the house may **herald** a new interest in rural history across the northern **hemisphere**. \"**Henceforth**,\" he says, \"we must protect the homes of ordinary people too.\""},"41":{"w":["hoarse","hoe","hoist","homely","homogeneous","hop","hose","hospitality","hostage","hound","hover","howl","huddle","hug","hull","hum","humanitarian","humanity","humidity","humiliation"],"title":"The Aid Ship","t":"Last autumn, my cousin joined the crew of a **humanitarian** ship carrying food and medicine to islands hit by a flood. The ship was old, and its steel **hull** was covered in rust, but the captain said it had survived worse storms. \"What matters is not the ship,\" he told the volunteers, \"but our sense of **humanity**.\"\n\nThe voyage was exhausting. The heat and **humidity** were almost unbearable, and the crew worked long hours loading boxes. Each morning, sailors would **hoist** the flag and check the engines, while volunteers cleaned the deck with a **hose**. The engines would **hum** day and night, and my cousin soon learned to sleep through the noise.\n\nOn the third night, a storm struck. The wind began to **howl**, and waves crashed over the deck. The passengers had to **huddle** together in the dining room, and a seabird tried to **hover** above the ship before giving up and flying away. \"For two days, we were **hostage** to the weather,\" my cousin said. By the end, many of the crew were **hoarse** from shouting over the wind.\n\nWhen the ship finally arrived, the islanders greeted them with incredible **hospitality**. Although they had lost almost everything, they shared their food and invited the crew into their **homely** wooden houses. Children would **hop** onto the volunteers' backs, and old women would **hug** them in thanks. One farmer, holding a **hoe** he had saved from the flood, said he would start planting again the next day.\n\nThe trip changed my cousin. She had once suffered a deep **humiliation** at work, when her manager criticised her in front of everyone, and the memory had **hounded|hound** her for years. On the island, it no longer seemed important. She noticed that the islanders were a **homogeneous** community, sharing one language and one way of life, yet they welcomed strangers without hesitation."},"42":{"w":["hurl","hurrah","hurricane","hush","hydraulic","hydrocarbon","hymn","hypothesis","hysteria","hysterical","icy","idealism","ideally","ideology","idiot","idleness","ignite","ignorance","illiterate","illuminate"],"title":"The Night of the Hurricane","t":"Three years ago, a powerful **hurricane** struck the coastal town where my friend Rosa lives. Forecasters had warned that it would be the strongest in decades, but many people refused to leave their homes. \"There was a kind of **hysteria** on the news,\" Rosa remembers, \"so some people simply stopped believing it.\"\n\nThe storm arrived at night. Wind would **hurl** branches against the windows, and rain fell so heavily that the streets turned into rivers. When the power failed, Rosa's family lit candles to **illuminate** the room, and her grandmother sang an old **hymn** to calm the children. \"**Hush**,\" she whispered, \"it will pass.\"\n\nRosa's neighbour, an elderly man who was **illiterate**, had not understood the written warnings and stayed at home. When his roof collapsed, Rosa's brother rushed out to help him, ignoring the **icy** rain. His mother called him an **idiot** for taking such a risk, but she was secretly proud. When he returned safely with the old man, the whole family gave a loud \"**hurrah**.\"\n\nAfter the storm, scientists studied what had gone wrong. One **hypothesis** was that a leak of **hydrocarbon** fuel from a damaged factory had caused a fire to **ignite** near the harbour. Engineers also found that the town's **hydraulic** flood gates had failed because of poor maintenance.\n\nThe disaster sparked fierce debate. Some blamed **ignorance** and **idleness** among officials, who had ignored repeated warnings. Others argued that the problem was **ideology**: politicians cared more about cutting taxes than protecting people. Rosa, a student of environmental science, still believes in **idealism**. \"**Ideally**, we would prevent such disasters,\" she says. Her younger sister became almost **hysterical** with laughter when Rosa announced that she would run for mayor one day, but Rosa was serious."},"43":{"w":["imaginative","imitation","immerse","immigration","immortal","immune","immunity","impair","impart","impartial","imperative","imperial","impetus","implicit","implore","impoverish","improper","impulse","impurity","inaccessible"],"title":"The Language Teacher","t":"Ms. Kim came to China from Korea twenty years ago and now teaches Chinese to new immigrants in a large southern city. Her own experience of **immigration** was hard. She arrived with little money and spoke almost no Chinese. \"I felt as if I had fallen into a deep sea,\" she says. \"The only way to survive was to **immerse** myself completely.\"\n\nToday she runs a small language school famous for its **imaginative** teaching methods. Instead of memorising lists, students cook meals, visit markets and act out conversations. She believes that **imitation** is the first step in learning a language: \"Children copy sounds before they understand them. Adults should do the same.\" Mistakes, she adds, are like an **impurity** in water: with time and movement, they settle and disappear.\n\nShe also teaches culture. Her students visit the **imperial** palace in Beijing, and she explains the **implicit** rules of Chinese politeness, such as refusing a gift once before accepting it. She warns them that using the wrong words in formal situations can seem **improper**, even if the grammar is correct.\n\nMs. Kim believes it is **imperative** to give new immigrants practical help. Many arrive **impoverished|impoverish**, and some live in areas almost **inaccessible** by public transport. She often visits them herself. Once, a student's mother **implored|implore** her to help find a doctor for her sick son, whose **immune** system had been weakened by illness. Ms. Kim found a clinic that offered free care and helped the boy rebuild his **immunity**.\n\nHer teaching is **impartial**: rich or poor, every student receives the same attention. She says that fear and stress can **impair** learning, so her classroom is relaxed. \"A teacher should **impart** confidence as well as knowledge,\" she explains. Her **impulse** to help others gives her school its **impetus**. A writer once described her as \"an **immortal** spirit in a tired city.\" She laughs: \"I'm just a teacher.\""},"44":{"w":["inaccurate","inadequate","inasmuch","inaugurate","incandescent","incapable","incense","incentive","incidence","incidentally","inclination","inclusive","incompatible","incomplete","incorporate","incredible","incur","indefinitely","indicative","indignation"],"title":"Lighting the Old Street","t":"Last spring, our city decided to restore an old shopping street that had been neglected for years. The street's lamps were still **incandescent** bulbs from the 1980s, and many of them no longer worked. Shop owners complained that the lighting was **inadequate** and that customers felt unsafe at night.\n\nThe city council offered an **incentive**: shop owners who renovated their buildings would receive a tax reduction. The plan aimed to **incorporate** modern technology into the street's traditional design. Engineers proposed solar-powered lamps, but they soon found that the new system was **incompatible** with the old electrical wiring.\n\nThe project faced many problems. Early cost estimates proved **inaccurate**, and the city had to **incur** extra expenses. Some contractors were **incapable** of finishing their work on time, and one section of the street remained **incomplete** for months. Residents expressed **indignation** when the council suggested delaying the project **indefinitely**.\n\nLocal shop owners were divided. Some were **incensed|incense** by the noise and dust; others showed a strong **inclination** to cooperate. A tea shop owner, who burned **incense** every morning for good luck, said he trusted the project. \"**Inasmuch** as it brings more customers,\" he said, \"I support it.\"\n\nFinally, the mayor came to **inaugurate** the new street. The opening ceremony was **inclusive**, with performances by children, elderly dancers and migrant workers. The results were **incredible**: crime fell, and the **incidence** of road accidents dropped by half. The success is **indicative** of what careful planning can achieve. **Incidentally**, the tea shop owner now sells twice as much tea as before, and he says the new lights are the best luck he has ever had."},"45":{"w":["indiscriminately","induce","induction","indulge","industrious","inertia","inevitably","infancy","infectious","inference","inferiority","infinitely","infinity","inflate","inflation","inflict","infrastructure","infusion","ingenious","ingenuity"],"title":"The Village Inventor","t":"In a small village in Henan, a farmer named Mr. Gao has become famous for his **ingenious** inventions. He has no formal education beyond primary school, but his **ingenuity** has improved the lives of hundreds of families. His workshop is full of strange machines made from bicycle parts, old engines and pieces of wood.\n\nHis first invention came from necessity. When rising **inflation** made fertiliser too expensive, he designed a machine that turns kitchen waste into compost. Later, he built a simple pump that could **inflate** tyres using wind power. \"Problems are like **infinity**,\" he jokes. \"There's always one more.\"\n\nMr. Gao is extremely **industrious**. He wakes at five every morning and works until late at night. His **infectious** enthusiasm has inspired many young villagers to start their own projects. He believes that a feeling of **inferiority** often holds rural people back. \"City people think we can't invent anything,\" he says. \"That's a wrong **inference**.\"\n\nThe village's **infrastructure** was poor when he was young. Roads were muddy, and electricity was unreliable. Officials used to spray chemicals **indiscriminately** on the fields, which would **inflict** damage on the soil. Mr. Gao invented a small sprayer that uses much less chemical and has helped to **induce** better farming habits.\n\nHis success has attracted attention. A university invited him to give a lecture as part of its **induction** programme for engineering students. A recent **infusion** of government money will allow him to build a proper workshop. He admits that he sometimes **indulges|indulge** in buying unnecessary parts. \"Change is slow,\" he says, \"because people suffer from **inertia**. But **inevitably**, good ideas spread.\" Rural innovation, he believes, is still in its **infancy**, but its potential is **infinitely** greater than people imagine."},"46":{"w":["inherent","inhibit","initially","initiate","inject","inland","inlet","innate","innovation","innumerable","inorganic","insane","insanity","insidious","insistent","inspector","inspiration","instability","installment","instantaneous"],"title":"The Lake Inspector","t":"Mr. Ye works as an environmental **inspector** at a large **inland** lake in central China. Every morning, he takes a small boat out to collect water samples from each **inlet** where rivers enter the lake. His job is to protect a water source used by **innumerable** families, farms and factories.\n\n**Initially**, the lake's problems were easy to see: dead fish, green water and a terrible smell. Today, the danger is more **insidious**. Many pollutants are invisible, including **inorganic** chemicals from industry that build up slowly in the food chain. \"The risk is **inherent** in modern life,\" Mr. Ye explains. \"We use thousands of chemicals and know little about most of them.\"\n\nHe has an **innate** curiosity and loves solving problems. Years ago, he proposed a plan to **inject** beneficial bacteria into the lake's sediment to break down pollution. Many colleagues thought the idea was **insane**. \"They said it was pure **insanity**,\" he laughs. But he was **insistent**, and the government finally agreed to **initiate** a small trial.\n\nThe results were encouraging. The bacteria seemed to **inhibit** the growth of harmful algae, and water quality improved within a year. The project became an important **innovation** in lake management, and other provinces began to study it. The equipment was expensive, so the local government paid for it in monthly **installments|installment**.\n\nChallenges remain. Climate change has created **instability** in the lake's ecosystem, with sudden floods and droughts. New sensors now give **instantaneous** readings of water quality, sending alerts to Mr. Ye's phone whenever something goes wrong. He says his greatest **inspiration** comes from the fishermen who have lived beside the lake for generations. \"They taught me that the lake is alive,\" he says. \"Our job is to keep it that way.\""},"47":{"w":["instinctively","instructor","instrumental","insulate","insulator","intact","intake","integral","integrity","intellect","intellectually","intelligible","intensify","intent","interact","interconnect","intercourse","interface","interim","intermittent"],"title":"Learning to Fly","t":"Last year, my friend Hui decided to fulfil a childhood dream: she began training to become a pilot. Her **instructor**, a former air force officer named Captain Ma, has taught hundreds of students. \"Flying is not just about **intellect**,\" he told her on the first day. \"It's about judgement and **integrity**.\"\n\nThe training was demanding. Hui had to learn how the plane's systems **interact** and how every part is **interconnected|interconnect**. She studied the cockpit **interface** until she could find each switch with her eyes closed. She learned how electrical wires are wrapped in an **insulator** and why engineers **insulate** the cabin against extreme cold.\n\nAt first, the radio messages were barely **intelligible** to her. Air traffic controllers spoke quickly and used technical codes. But slowly she learned the language of aviation, which Captain Ma described as \"the international **intercourse** of the skies.\" Clear communication, he said, is **integral** to safety.\n\nHui found the course **intellectually** challenging but deeply rewarding. She was **intent** on passing every test the first time. Captain Ma pushed her hard, and his training became more difficult each week. \"We must **intensify** the pressure,\" he said, \"so that you stay calm when it really matters.\"\n\nOne day, during a practice flight, the engine made an **intermittent** noise. Hui **instinctively** checked the fuel **intake** and found a small blockage. She landed safely, and the plane was **intact**. Captain Ma praised her calm response, which he said was **instrumental** in avoiding an accident. While the plane was repaired, the school gave her an **interim** licence to continue training on another aircraft. She now hopes to fly passenger jets within three years."},"48":{"w":["intersection","intervene","intervention","intonation","intricacy","intricate","intrigue","intriguing","intrinsic","intuition","invalid","invaluable","invariably","inventory","inversely","invert","invoice","ion","ironically","irony"],"title":"The Clockmaker's Shop","t":"At a busy **intersection** in the old city, there is a tiny shop that repairs antique clocks. Its owner, Mr. Pei, has worked there for forty years. His windows are filled with **intricate** brass machines, and the sound of a hundred ticking clocks fills the room. Visitors often find the place **intriguing**; it feels like stepping back in time.\n\nMr. Pei says the **intricacy** of old clocks never stops amazing him. Each one contains hundreds of tiny parts that must work together perfectly. He keeps a detailed **inventory** of spare parts, many of which can no longer be found anywhere else. His knowledge is **invaluable** to collectors across the country.\n\nHe relies on **intuition** as much as skill. \"When a clock is sick, I can hear it,\" he says. \"Its rhythm changes, like the **intonation** of a person's voice.\" He explains that the speed of a clock is **inversely** related to the length of its pendulum: the longer the pendulum, the slower it swings. Some repairs require him to **invert** the clock and work on it upside down.\n\nCustomers **invariably** ask whether their clocks are valuable. Mr. Pei tells them that a clock's **intrinsic** value lies in its craftsmanship, not its price. **Ironically**, the most expensive clock he ever repaired was also the least accurate. He enjoys the **irony**.\n\nThe shop has survived many difficulties. When the city planned to demolish the building, residents had to **intervene**, and their **intervention** saved it. A modern watch company once tried to **intrigue** him with a job offer, but he refused. He still writes every **invoice** by hand, and he warns customers that a warranty becomes **invalid** if they open the clock themselves. His grandson, a chemistry student, jokes that he understands every **ion** in the air but nothing about clocks."},"49":{"w":["irregularity","irrespective","irrigation","irritate","ivory","jack","jealousy","jean","jelly","jeopardize","jerk","jingle","jog","jug","junction","junk","justification","kernel","kidnap","kidney"],"title":"The Harvest Market","t":"Every October, farmers from across the county gather at a crossroads **junction** outside our town for the annual harvest market. It is a noisy, colourful event, full of stalls selling corn, beans, honey and homemade **jelly**. Children **jog** between the tables, and a band plays a cheerful **jingle** that everyone knows by heart.\n\nThis year's harvest was good, thanks to a new **irrigation** system built last spring. Before that, the fields depended on rain, and **irregularity** in rainfall often damaged crops. The new system brings water to every farm, **irrespective** of its size or location.\n\nThe market sells more than food. One stall offers old farm tools, including a rusty car **jack** and a pile of **junk** that a collector hopes to sell as antiques. An old woman sells clay **jugs|jug** decorated with flowers, and a young man sells bags made from recycled **jean** fabric. A sign warns customers not to buy **ivory**, which is illegal, and the police check every stall.\n\nNot everyone gets along. Some farmers feel a touch of **jealousy** when their neighbours win prizes for the largest pumpkin. Loud music can **irritate** older visitors, and one farmer argued that the crowds would **jeopardize** the safety of his animals. A rude driver was called a **jerk** after he tried to park in the middle of the market.\n\nThe best moment comes at the end of the day, when farmers roast corn over a fire and share the sweet **kernels|kernel** with everyone. Local children love a game in which they pretend to **kidnap** the scarecrow and hide it, while adults look for it. One doctor gives free health checks, warning people that too much salt can damage the **kidney**. For the organisers, the market's greatest **justification** is simple: it brings the whole community together."},"50":{"w":["kilowatt","kin","kindle","kinetic","kit","knight","lamentation","landscape","latent","lathe","latitude","lattice","lawsuit","layman","layoff","leaflet","leakage","lease","legacy","legitimate"],"title":"The Toymaker's Workshop","t":"Three years ago, my uncle lost his job during a wave of factory **layoffs|layoff**. At fifty-two, he worried that no one would hire him. Then he remembered a skill he had learned as a boy: woodworking. He decided to **lease** an empty workshop and start making wooden toys.\n\nHe bought an old **lathe** and a basic tool **kit**, and he began by making simple toys: horses, cars and a wooden **knight** on horseback. Soon he became more ambitious. He designed **kinetic** toys that move when children turn a handle, and a **lattice** of wooden gears that spins like a clock. The workshop uses only a few **kilowatts|kilowatt** of electricity a day, since much of the work is done by hand.\n\nHis success revealed a **latent** talent. He had never thought of himself as an artist, but customers praised the beauty of his work. His small business began to **kindle** interest among young people in the town, and several asked to become his apprentices.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. A **leakage** of water from the roof damaged several toys, and the owner of the building refused to pay for repairs. My uncle considered a **lawsuit** but finally decided to settle the matter peacefully. As a **layman** in legal matters, he found the process confusing. He also faced questions about whether his business was **legitimate**, since he had no formal licence at first.\n\nToday, the workshop is thriving. My uncle prints a **leaflet** describing his toys and hands it out at local markets. He wants to leave a **legacy** for his **kin**, and his daughter now helps him run the business. When the old factory finally closed, there was much **lamentation** in the town, but my uncle's workshop shows that the economic **landscape** can change in unexpected ways. \"You have more **latitude** when you work for yourself,\" he says. \"And more happiness.\""},"51":{"w":["lengthen","leopard","liability","lighter","likeness","lily","limestone","limp","linear","linger","linguistic","lining","lipstick","literacy","literal","literally","litter","lobby","locality","locust"],"title":"The Cave Guide","t":"In the hills of Guangxi, where **limestone** peaks rise like green towers from the fields, an old man named Mr. Wei guides tourists through a famous cave. He walks with a slight **limp**, the result of a climbing accident in his youth, but he knows every passage by heart.\n\nThe cave stretches in an almost **linear** path for three kilometres underground. Mr. Wei carries a torch and an old **lighter**, which he uses to light candles at a small shrine near the entrance. Visitors often **linger** there, admiring rock formations that bear a strange **likeness** to animals and people. One looks like a sleeping **leopard**; another resembles a bunch of **lilies|lily**.\n\nMr. Wei speaks the local dialect, and he loves to explain the **linguistic** history of the area. Many place names, he says, should not be taken in a **literal** sense. \"Dragon Mountain does not **literally** contain a dragon,\" he jokes. Despite little schooling, he taught himself to read and now runs evening **literacy** classes for older villagers.\n\nTourism has brought both money and problems. Visitors sometimes **litter** the cave with plastic bottles, and Mr. Wei collects bags of rubbish every week. Last year, a hotel company tried to **lobby** the government to build a large resort nearby. Mr. Wei and his neighbours opposed the plan, arguing that it would damage the **locality**. The company also worried about legal **liability** if tourists were injured.\n\nThe region has faced natural threats too. Old people remember a summer when **locusts|locust** destroyed the crops. Today, Mr. Wei's daughter runs a small shop at the hotel **lobby**, selling scarves with silk **lining** and **lipstick** made from local flowers. The government plans to **lengthen** the tourist path next year, but Mr. Wei hopes it will not change the cave's quiet beauty."},"52":{"w":["lodging","lofty","logistics","longevity","longing","longitude","loosely","lounge","luminous","luncheon","luxurious","magician","magistrate","magnetism","magnify","magnitude","maiden","majesty","makeup","malice"],"title":"A Weekend at the Grand Hotel","t":"Last summer, my grandparents celebrated their sixtieth wedding anniversary with a weekend at a famous old hotel in Qingdao. Built in 1910, the hotel is known for its **luxurious** rooms and its **lofty** ceilings painted with stars. It has hosted presidents, film stars and even a visiting queen, whose **majesty** is still remembered in a portrait in the main hall.\n\nMy cousin organised the trip, and the **logistics** were complicated. She had to book **lodging** for twelve family members, arrange transport for my grandfather's wheelchair and plan a special **luncheon** on the terrace. The menu was **loosely** based on dishes my grandparents ate on their wedding day.\n\nThe hotel's **lounge** became our favourite place. In the evenings, a **magician** entertained guests with card tricks, and his personal **magnetism** kept even the children quiet. He performed one trick using a **magnifying|magnify** glass that made a coin seem to disappear. My grandmother, who never wears **makeup**, laughed like a young girl.\n\nOver dinner, my grandfather told stories from his career as a **magistrate**. He handled thousands of cases and said that he rarely saw true **malice**; most crimes, he believed, came from poverty or desperation. He also described the **magnitude** of the changes he has seen in his lifetime, from oil lamps to smartphones.\n\nOn the last evening, we walked along the beach. The sea was **luminous** with tiny glowing creatures, and my grandmother pointed at a map in the hotel showing the city's **longitude** and latitude. She told us how they had met on the **maiden** voyage of a ferry across the bay. Asked about the secret of their **longevity** and happiness, she smiled. \"We always had a **longing** to see each other,\" she said. \"Even after sixty years.\""},"53":{"w":["malicious","mall","malpractice","mammal","managerial","manifest","manipulate","mansion","manuscript","maple","marathon","marginal","maritime","marsh","marshal","martyr","marvel","masculine","masculinity","massacre"],"title":"The Writer's Mansion","t":"On the edge of a coastal town stands a red brick **mansion** surrounded by **maple** trees. It was the home of a famous novelist who wrote about **maritime** life in the early twentieth century. Today, it is a museum, and visitors can see the desk where he wrote, along with the original **manuscript** of his greatest novel.\n\nThe novel tells the story of a fishing family living beside a **marsh**, where birds and small **mammals|mammal** shared the land with people. Critics **marvel** at the way the writer described nature. His work also explored ideas of **masculinity**: his male characters are strong and **masculine** on the surface but deeply vulnerable inside.\n\nThe writer's life was not peaceful. Rivals spread **malicious** rumours about him, and some newspapers tried to **manipulate** public opinion against his books. His health suffered after a doctor's **malpractice** left him partly deaf. Yet his talent continued to **manifest** itself in every new book.\n\nHis most powerful chapter describes a historical **massacre** in the town during a war, when soldiers killed dozens of fishermen. The writer treated the victims as **martyrs|martyr** and honoured them with dignity. A memorial stone now stands in the town square.\n\nThe museum has faced its own struggles. A developer once planned to replace it with a shopping **mall**, and the museum's **managerial** team fought for years to save it. Its income is **marginal**, so it depends on donations. Every spring, the town holds a **marathon** that ends at the mansion's gate, and a race **marshal** in a bright jacket guides runners through the final kilometre."},"54":{"w":["mast","mastery","maternity","maturation","maturity","meaningful","mechanism","mediate","medieval","meditate","melancholy","melody","membership","menace","merchandise","merciful","merge","mesh","metabolic","metallic"],"title":"Music in the Maternity Ward","t":"At a large hospital in Chengdu, a music therapist named Ms. Hu works with patients in the **maternity** ward. She believes that a gentle **melody** can calm anxious mothers and help newborn babies sleep. Her work combines art and science, and doctors say it has a **meaningful** effect on patients' wellbeing.\n\nMs. Hu studied both music and medicine. She explains the **mechanism** behind her work: certain rhythms slow the heart rate and may even influence **metabolic** activity. She often plays **medieval** songs on a small harp, because their simple patterns seem to **merge** naturally with the sound of breathing.\n\nHer path to **mastery** was long. She practised for years before she felt ready to play in hospitals. She also learned to **meditate**, which helps her stay calm when patients are in pain. \"Music must **mesh** with the mood of the room,\" she says. \"Sometimes the right note is silence.\"\n\nMany new mothers feel tired or **melancholy** after giving birth. Ms. Hu's sessions give them a moment of peace. One mother described the music as \"a **merciful** gift.\" Doctors also note that the babies' **maturation** seems to benefit from regular exposure to soft sounds, though more research is needed.\n\nNot everyone supported the programme at first. Some staff thought noise was a **menace** to patients' rest, and Ms. Hu had to **mediate** between doctors and nurses with different views. Today, the hospital has a music club, and its **membership** includes doctors, patients and volunteers. The gift shop sells **merchandise** such as CDs and small **metallic** bells. Outside the ward, a flag flies from a tall **mast** in the garden. Ms. Hu says her work has taught her emotional **maturity**: \"Every life begins with sound. I want it to be beautiful.\""},"55":{"w":["metallurgy","meteorite","metropolitan","microprocessor","microscopic","microwave","mighty","migrant","migrate","militant","milky","millionaire","mingle","miniature","minimal","minimize","mint","mischief","miser","misery"],"title":"The Millionaire Who Lived Like a Miser","t":"For forty years, an old man named Mr. Qian lived in a tiny flat in a busy **metropolitan** district of Shanghai. Neighbours thought he was poor. He wore old clothes, cooked simple meals in a **microwave** and bought only the cheapest vegetables. Children joked that he was a **miser** who counted every coin. When he died last year, everyone was shocked to learn that he was a **millionaire**.\n\nMr. Qian had been an engineer specialising in **metallurgy**. In the 1980s, he helped design a new kind of **microprocessor** for factory machines, and he invested his savings wisely. Yet he spent **minimal** amounts on himself, preferring to **minimize** his needs. \"Comfort is not happiness,\" he once told a neighbour.\n\nHis will contained many surprises. He left most of his money to help **migrant** workers who **migrate** from the countryside to find jobs in the city. Many of them live in **misery**, he wrote, working long hours for little pay. He also donated a rare **meteorite** to a science museum, along with a collection of **microscopic** fossils.\n\nNeighbours remembered his kindness. He rarely joined in social events, preferring not to **mingle** with crowds, but he often helped children with their homework. He was tolerant of their **mischief**, laughing when they played tricks on him. He kept a **miniature** garden on his balcony, growing **mint** and small flowers.\n\nSome local activists, known for their **militant** campaigns against inequality, praised his generosity as a **mighty** example to the rich. His funeral was simple. Mourners drank **milky** tea in a small hall, and someone read his favourite line: \"A life is measured not by what we keep, but by what we give.\""},"56":{"w":["misfortune","missionary","mitten","mixer","mob","mobility","mobilize","mock","moderately","modesty","modification","modulate","module","molecular","momentary","momentum","monarch","monetary","monk","monopoly"],"title":"The Mountain Monastery","t":"High in the mountains of Sichuan stands a Buddhist monastery that was built eight hundred years ago by order of a **monarch**. For centuries it was home to about fifty **monks|monk**, but by the 1990s only three remained. Today, it has become a small guesthouse, and visitors come from all over the world to experience its quiet way of life.\n\nThe change began with **misfortune**. In 2008, an earthquake damaged the main hall, and the monks lacked the **monetary** resources to repair it. The head monk, a man known for his **modesty**, decided to **mobilize** help from local villagers and volunteers. Within months, the **momentum** of the project had grown, and donations arrived from around the country.\n\nGuests must follow simple rules. They wake at five, eat **moderately** and spend time in silence. In winter, the temperature falls far below zero, and visitors wear thick **mittens|mitten** while walking in the courtyard. The monks teach a short **module** on meditation, showing guests how to **modulate** their breathing.\n\nNot everyone respects the monastery's peace. Some tourists arrive in a noisy **mob**, taking photos everywhere, and a few even **mock** the monks' customs. The head monk responds with patience. \"Their anger is **momentary**,\" he says. \"Peace lasts longer.\"\n\nThe monastery has an interesting history. A Western **missionary** stayed there in the 1920s and wrote a book about the monks' lives. More recently, a **molecular** biologist spent a summer there studying the effects of meditation on the brain. Small **modifications|modification** have made the buildings safer, and a new road has improved **mobility** for elderly visitors. But the monks have refused offers from tour companies seeking a **monopoly** on bookings. A sound engineer once recorded their chanting using a portable **mixer**, and the recording has been played around the world."},"57":{"w":["monster","monstrous","moor","mop","morality","mortgage","mosque","moss","motorway","mountainous","multiplication","multitude","municipal","murmur","muscular","muse","mustard","myth","naive","napkin"],"title":"A House on the Moor","t":"Five years ago, my aunt and her husband left their jobs in London and bought an old stone cottage on a windswept **moor** in northern England. To pay for it, they took out a large **mortgage**, and friends thought they were **naive** to leave the city. \"We were tired of traffic, offices and the **motorway**,\" my aunt says. \"We wanted space.\"\n\nThe landscape is wild and **mountainous**, covered with heather, rocks and soft green **moss**. The nearest town is ten kilometres away, with a church, a **mosque**, a market and a small **municipal** library. My uncle, who is tall and **muscular**, does most of the heavy work, repairing walls and chopping wood. My aunt spends her mornings painting, finding her **muse** in the changing light over the hills.\n\nLocal people love to tell stories. One old **myth** claims that a **monster** lives in the lake below the cottage, a **monstrous** black dog with glowing eyes. My aunt laughs at the tale, but she admits that the wind sometimes sounds like a **murmur** of voices at night.\n\nLife on the moor has taught them a different **morality** of time. They no longer rush; they eat slowly, using cloth **napkins|napkin** and homemade **mustard** made from an old family recipe. On rainy days, my uncle has to **mop** the kitchen floor when water comes in under the door.\n\nTheir daughter attends the village school, where there are only twelve pupils. She is learning **multiplication** tables and the names of birds, and she can identify a **multitude** of wild flowers. \"I used to think the countryside was boring,\" she says. \"Now I know it's full of life.\""},"58":{"w":["narration","narrative","nave","necessitate","negligible","negotiate","negotiation","neighbouring","neuron","neutron","nickel","nickname","nightmare","nominal","nominate","nonetheless","norm","normalization","northward","northwards"],"title":"A Scientist's Story","t":"Last year, a famous neuroscientist, Professor Lin, published her memoirs. The book's **narrative** follows her from a poor childhood in a small village to a career at one of the world's top universities. Her clear, honest **narration** has made the book a bestseller, and she was recently **nominated|nominate** for a national literary prize.\n\nShe grew up in a family of scientists. Her father was a physicist who studied the behaviour of the **neutron**, and he encouraged her to ask questions about everything. As a child, she earned the **nickname** \"Little Why\" because of her endless curiosity. Her family moved **northward** several times for her father's work, and she attended schools in four provinces.\n\nHer own research focuses on the **neuron**, the basic cell of the brain. She studies how memories are formed and lost. One chapter describes the **nightmare** of losing years of data when her laboratory flooded, an experience that would **necessitate** starting several projects again. The financial loss was not **negligible**: it cost millions of yuan.\n\nShe also writes about life as a woman in science. When she began her career, it was the **norm** for women to leave research after marriage. She refused. Her salary was **nominal** at first, but she continued **nonetheless**. Later, she had to **negotiate** with university leaders for equal pay, and the **negotiation** lasted two years.\n\nThe book ends with her return to her home village. She describes walking **northwards** along a river to her old school, which was once a church; she remembers studying in its **nave**. She mentions the **normalization** of relations between China and **neighbouring** countries, which allowed scientists to collaborate across borders. The final page describes an old **nickel** coin her father gave her, which she still carries for luck."},"59":{"w":["notable","notation","noted","notification","notorious","notwithstanding","nought","nourish","nourishment","novelty","numerical","nun","nurture","nutrient","nutrition","oak","oar","oath","obedience","obedient"],"title":"The Convent Garden","t":"On a hillside in southern France, a group of elderly **nuns|nun** runs a large vegetable garden behind their convent. They have lived there for decades, following a life of prayer, work and **obedience**. When they entered the convent, each nun took an **oath** to live simply and serve others.\n\nThe garden is **notable** for its beauty and productivity. Rows of vegetables stand beneath an ancient **oak** tree, and herbs grow along the stone walls. The sisters believe that good soil must be **nourished|nourish** with compost, not chemicals. \"If we **nurture** the earth,\" says Sister Marie, the oldest nun and a **noted** expert on organic farming, \"it will feed us.\"\n\nNutrition is central to their work. They grow food rich in every important **nutrient**, and they give vegetables to poor families in the village. A local doctor says the convent's food has improved **nutrition** in the area and provided valuable **nourishment** for children. Sister Marie keeps detailed records of every harvest, using an old **numerical** **notation** she learned as a girl.\n\nThe convent has a long history. In the eighteenth century, a **notorious** thief hid there from the police, and the nuns gave him shelter. **Notwithstanding** his crimes, they believed everyone deserved kindness. Today, visitors find the convent a **novelty**, and some arrive by boat, rowing with a single **oar** across the lake.\n\nThe nuns are cheerful and practical. Sister Marie laughs when visitors ask whether she is always **obedient**. \"Not always,\" she admits. \"God knows I argue.\" When the local government sent an official **notification** that the convent's land might be sold for development, the sisters refused to give up. \"We have nothing to lose,\" Sister Marie said. \"We started with **nought**, and we will end with nought.\""},"60":{"w":["obese","obscene","obsession","obstinate","odor","offensive","offset","offspring","ohm","olive","onward","opaque","opium","optimal","optimism","optimum","orchard","ordinarily","orientation","ornamental"],"title":"The Olive Orchard","t":"On a sunny hillside in southern Spain, a woman named Elena runs an **olive** **orchard** that has belonged to her family for six generations. Some of the trees are over five hundred years old, with twisted trunks and silver-green leaves. Visitors often mistake them for **ornamental** trees, but they produce some of the finest oil in the region.\n\nElena's love of olives began in childhood and soon became an **obsession**. She studied agriculture at university and returned home determined to improve the farm. \"**Ordinarily**, young people leave the countryside,\" she says. \"I came back.\" Her parents were **obstinate** at first, refusing to change their traditional methods, but they gradually accepted her ideas.\n\nShe has worked hard to find the **optimal** conditions for her trees. The **orientation** of the slope, facing south, gives the trees **optimum** sunlight. She tests the soil regularly, and she uses solar panels to power the irrigation pumps; she jokes that she now understands more about **ohms|ohm** and volts than about cooking.\n\nThe work is not always pleasant. During the harvest, the press room fills with a strong **odor** of crushed fruit, and the oil must be stored in **opaque** containers to protect it from light. Some buyers make **offensive** comments about women farmers, and wealthy companies offer **obscene** sums of money for the land. Elena always refuses.\n\nShe is also concerned about health. Many people in Europe are now **obese**, and she believes olive oil can be part of a healthier diet. Her family history includes darker chapters: an ancestor traded **opium** in the nineteenth century, a fact she discusses openly. Profits from oil now help **offset** the cost of new trees. Elena's **optimism** is infectious. She hopes her **offspring** will continue the work. \"Olives teach patience,\" she says. \"From here, we can only move **onward**.\""},"61":{"w":["orthodox","outbreak","outcry","outermost","outlaw","outrage","overestimate","overflow","overhang","overhear","overlap","overload","overt","overthrow","overwhelm","overwhelming","oxide","oxidize","oyster","pal"],"title":"The Oyster Crisis","t":"For generations, families on a group of small islands off the east coast have made their living by farming **oysters|oyster**. The **outermost** island, where cliffs **overhang** the sea, is famous for producing the finest shellfish in the region. But three years ago, a sudden **outbreak** of disease killed almost half the oysters, and the islanders were desperate.\n\nScientists soon discovered the cause. During heavy rains, a sewage system on the mainland would **overflow**, sending waste into the sea. A nearby factory had also released metal **oxide** waste, and old iron cages in the water had begun to **oxidize**, adding rust to the polluted mix. The system had been under heavy **overload** for years, but nobody had acted.\n\nThe discovery caused a public **outcry**. Fishermen expressed their **outrage** at a meeting with officials, and newspapers accused the government of **overt** neglect. Different agencies had **overlapping|overlap** responsibilities, and each blamed the others. One fisherman claimed he had happened to **overhear** an official admitting that warnings had been ignored.\n\nThe scale of the damage was **overwhelming**. Many families lost their entire income, and the stress began to **overwhelm** the community. Some experts warned that people should not **overestimate** how quickly the sea could recover.\n\nEventually, the government acted. It **outlawed|outlaw** the dumping of industrial waste and spent millions upgrading the sewage system. Some young farmers rejected **orthodox** methods and began raising oysters in floating baskets, which proved healthier. One islander joked that the crisis had helped them **overthrow** a hundred years of bad habits. My **pal** Jun, whose family farms oysters, says business has finally returned to normal. \"We learned that the sea is not a rubbish bin,\" he says."},"62":{"w":["pamphlet","panic","panther","pantry","parachute","paradox","paradoxically","paralysis","paralyze","parameter","participant","partition","pasture","patent","pathetic","patriot","patriotic","patrol","patron","payoff"],"title":"The Night the Panther Escaped","t":"Last summer, a black **panther** escaped from a wildlife park near our town. For one night, there was **panic** in the neighbourhood. Police officers began to **patrol** the streets, and the park staff handed out a **pamphlet** telling residents to stay indoors and keep pets inside.\n\nThe escape seemed like a **paradox**: the park had recently installed new fences designed to the highest safety standards. **Paradoxically**, the problem was not the fence itself but a wooden **partition** inside the animal house, which had rotted during the rainy season. The panther simply pushed it aside and walked out.\n\nThe park's director, Mr. Liang, is a former soldier and a proud **patriot** who once served in a **patriotic** youth organisation. He spent the night leading the search. Every **participant** in the search team had to follow strict rules, and the search area was defined by careful **parameters|parameter**. A rescue team even prepared to drop supplies by **parachute** into the forest if the search continued for days.\n\nThe panther was finally found at dawn, hiding in a farmer's **pantry**, eating a bag of dried meat. It was calm and sleepy. A vet used a dart to **paralyze** it temporarily, and the **paralysis** wore off a few hours later, once the animal was safely back in its enclosure. The farmer was shocked but laughed about it later. \"My dog was **pathetic**,\" he said. \"He slept through the whole thing.\"\n\nThe incident had an unexpected **payoff**. Publicity attracted thousands of new visitors, and a wealthy **patron** donated money for new buildings. A local engineer even received a **patent** for a tracking collar inspired by the escape. The panther now lives in a larger enclosure beside an open **pasture**, where children can safely watch it from a viewing platform."},"63":{"w":["peacock","peck","peculiarity","pedal","pedestrian","pedlar","peer","penalty","pendulum","penetration","perception","perch","perfection","peril","perimeter","periodic","periodical","peripheral","perish","permeate"],"title":"Cycling Through Old Villages","t":"Last spring, I joined a group of friends on a cycling tour through the villages of southern Anhui. We spent a week riding along narrow country roads, and by the end, my legs ached from pushing the **pedals|pedal** up endless hills. It was one of the best trips of my life.\n\nEvery village had its own **peculiarity**. In one, a **peacock** walked freely through the streets, spreading its tail for tourists. In another, chickens would **peck** at grain in the square while an old **pedlar** sold sweets from a wooden box. Birds would **perch** on the roofs of white houses with black tiles, and the smell of tea seemed to **permeate** the air.\n\nOur guide warned us about the **peril** of riding on busy roads. Trucks often ignore cyclists, and a **pedestrian** was injured on the highway the day before we arrived. Local police issue a heavy **penalty** for dangerous driving, but accidents still happen. We usually stayed on **peripheral** roads around the **perimeter** of the villages.\n\nThe trip changed my **perception** of the countryside. I had imagined it as a place of poverty, but I found communities full of life and pride. In one village, an old man showed us a clock whose **pendulum** had swung for a hundred years. He said **perfection** was not possible, but care was.\n\nOur guide was a keen reader who brought a **periodical** about rural history. He explained that the region suffers **periodic** floods, and many old buildings **perish** when rivers rise. He also told us about the **penetration** of modern technology: almost every farmer now has a smartphone. As we left, I turned to **peer** back at the misty hills, hoping to return one day."},"64":{"w":["permissible","perpendicular","perpetual","perplex","persecute","persevere","persistence","persistent","perspiration","persuasion","pertinent","pest","petition","petty","philanthropic","philanthropy","photoelectric","photography","physically","physiological"],"title":"The Persistent Photographer","t":"For thirty years, Mr. Jiang has used **photography** to document poverty in rural China. His pictures have appeared in newspapers around the world, and he has used the money from his work to support **philanthropic** projects in the villages he visits. \"**Philanthropy** is not about money,\" he says. \"It's about paying attention.\"\n\nHis work is **physically** demanding. He often walks for days through mountains carrying heavy equipment, and in summer his shirts are soaked with **perspiration**. Doctors who examined him found remarkable **physiological** strength for a man of sixty-five. He says the secret is simple: **persistence**.\n\nHe began his career in difficult times. In the 1980s, photographers who showed poverty were sometimes **persecuted|persecute** by officials who wanted to hide problems. Mr. Jiang was **persistent**, and he refused to stop. He believes it is never **permissible** to look away from suffering.\n\nHis methods are unusual. He refuses to use modern cameras with **photoelectric** sensors and still develops his film by hand. Many younger photographers are **perplexed|perplex** by his choice. He often shoots from strange angles, holding the camera **perpendicular** to the ground to capture tall mountain houses.\n\nOver the years, he has used **persuasion** rather than anger to change things. He started a **petition** asking the government to build schools in remote areas, and it gathered a million signatures. Critics once called his concerns **petty**, but his questions were always **pertinent**. He admits that some officials treat him as a **pest**. His life has been a **perpetual** struggle, but he says he will **persevere** as long as he can hold a camera."},"65":{"w":["pickle","pier","pilgrim","pineapple","pious","pirate","piston","plague","plateau","plausible","plea","plead","pledge","plump","plunder","plutocrat","pneumatic","polar","polarity","polymer"],"title":"The Pirate Museum","t":"On an old **pier** in a southern port city, there is a small museum dedicated to the history of **pirates|pirate** in the South China Sea. In the eighteenth century, pirate fleets would **plunder** merchant ships carrying silk, tea and spices. One famous pirate leader is said to have controlled more ships than the emperor's navy.\n\nThe museum tells many stories. Sailors on long voyages survived on rice, salted fish and **pickle**, and many died from disease. A **plague** once killed half the population of the port. Ships also carried **pious** **pilgrims|pilgrim** travelling to temples on distant islands, and some pilgrims wrote diaries describing pirate attacks. Merchants traded **pineapple** and sugar from the islands, and a few became so rich that historians describe them as **plutocrats|plutocrat**.\n\nOne exhibit describes the trial of a captured pirate. He made a **plea** for mercy, and his wife came to **plead** on his behalf. The judge accepted a **pledge** that he would never return to the sea. A portrait shows him as a **plump**, smiling man, not at all like the frightening figures of films. Historians say this version of events is **plausible**, though records are incomplete.\n\nThe museum also covers modern shipping. Visitors can see a steam engine with a giant **piston**, **pneumatic** tools used to repair ships and ropes made from strong **polymer** fibres. A special room describes the voyage of a research ship through **polar** waters to Antarctica.\n\nVisitor numbers have reached a **plateau** in recent years, and the director worries about the future. Opinions about pirates show a strange **polarity**: some visitors see them as criminals, others as romantic heroes. \"The truth is somewhere in between,\" the director says."},"66":{"w":["ponder","pope","popularity","porcelain","pore","portray","positively","postal","poster","postulate","poultry","prairie","preach","precede","precedent","precipitate","preclude","predecessor","predestine","prediction"],"title":"The Porcelain Collector","t":"Mr. Tang spent most of his life on a farm on the northern **prairie**, raising **poultry** and growing wheat. Yet his real passion lay far from the fields: he collected antique Chinese **porcelain**. Over fifty years, he gathered more than three hundred pieces, and his collection is now one of the most admired in the country.\n\nHis interest began by chance. As a boy, he saw a **poster** advertising a museum exhibition and persuaded his father to take him. He would later **ponder** for hours over a single blue-and-white vase. In the evenings, after work, he would **pore** over books and auction catalogues, learning to identify the age and origin of each piece.\n\nHe bought most of his collection by **postal** order, long before the internet made such purchases easy. Experts warned him that he could not assume every piece was genuine, and he learned to **postulate** a theory about each object before testing it. His early **predictions|prediction** about which styles would rise in value proved remarkably accurate, and the **popularity** of his favourite pieces grew rapidly.\n\nHis most famous object is a bowl once given as a gift to a **pope** in the sixteenth century. Historians say it was made by a master potter whose **predecessor** had designed bowls for the imperial court. The bowl's journey from China to Rome set a **precedent** for cultural exchange between East and West.\n\nMr. Tang never liked to **preach** about art. When journalists asked him to **portray** himself as an expert, he refused. \"I'm a farmer who loves beautiful things,\" he said. His decision to donate the collection to a public museum was **precipitated|precipitate** by a serious illness. He did not want age to **preclude** others from enjoying it. Some say his love for porcelain was **predestined|predestine**, since his grandfather had been a potter. Visitors who see the collection respond **positively**, and the museum hopes a new wing will **precede** a national tour."},"67":{"w":["predominant","predominantly","predominate","pregnancy","pregnant","premature","premise","premium","prerequisite","prescription","presentation","preservation","preset","preside","presidency","prestige","presume","pretext","prevailing","prevalence"],"title":"A Young Doctor's First Year","t":"Dr. Chen began her career last year in a county hospital in western China. Her medical degree was a **prerequisite** for the job, but she quickly learned that textbooks could not prepare her for everything. Her first month was spent mainly in the obstetrics department, where she cared for women during **pregnancy** and childbirth.\n\nMost of her patients were young farmers, and many arrived at the hospital only when they were already heavily **pregnant**. One night, a baby was born two months **premature**, weighing just over a kilogram. Dr. Chen and the nurses worked through the night to keep the baby warm and breathing. \"That was the moment I truly understood my job,\" she says.\n\nShe noticed that a few diseases **predominate** in the region. The **prevalence** of high blood pressure is especially worrying, and the **prevailing** diet, rich in salt and fat, makes the problem worse. Women are **predominantly** responsible for cooking, so Dr. Chen gives regular talks to village women about healthy eating. Writing a **prescription** is easy, she says, but changing habits is harder. Still, she never **presumes|presume** that patients will ignore her advice.\n\nThe hospital has limited resources. Patients must pay a **premium** for certain imported drugs, and the operating rooms are used on a **preset** schedule. Dr. Chen once gave a **presentation** to county officials asking for more equipment. The director of health, who would later win the **presidency** of a national medical association, was impressed.\n\nDr. Chen says that her **predominant** feeling is gratitude. She knows that rural work carries less **prestige** than a job in a big city hospital, and some colleagues use any **pretext** to transfer away. But she believes the **premise** of medicine is to serve those in need. Next year, she will **preside** over a new programme for the **preservation** of traditional herbal knowledge."},"68":{"w":["prevalent","prevention","prey","prick","primate","principally","printer","prism","privacy","probe","proceeding","producer","productivity","proficiency","proficient","profile","profound","prohibition","projecting","projector"],"title":"Filming the Forest Primates","t":"Last year, a television **producer** named Ms. Kong spent six months in the rainforests of Borneo filming a documentary about orangutans and other **primates|primate**. The project was **principally** funded by an international wildlife charity, and the finished film has won several awards for its **profound** portrait of animal life.\n\nThe filming was difficult. The team had to carry heavy equipment through thick forest, where thorns would **prick** their hands and leeches attached themselves to their legs. Cameras were hidden in trees, with **projecting** branches providing natural cover. The team respected the animals' **privacy** and never approached too closely.\n\nMs. Kong learned a great deal about primate behaviour. She watched mothers teach their young to find food and avoid becoming **prey** to snakes and wild cats. Scientists on the team used a small electronic **probe** to measure temperature and humidity in the nests. Their data revealed that illegal logging is **prevalent** in the region, threatening many species.\n\nThe film also explores human efforts at **prevention**. Local rangers enforce a strict **prohibition** on hunting, and the charity runs programmes to increase farmers' **productivity** so that they do not need to clear more forest. One ranger, **proficient** in three languages, became the film's guide; his **proficiency** with local dialects helped the team build trust with villagers.\n\nAfter filming, Ms. Kong spent months editing. She built a rough cut using a **projector** in a small studio, showing the footage on a white wall. Light from a glass **prism** on the windowsill sometimes scattered colours across the images. When the charity's **printer** produced the first posters, her **profile** appeared beside a baby orangutan. The film was screened at the **proceedings|proceeding** of an international conservation conference."},"69":{"w":["prolong","prominence","promotion","promptly","prone","propaganda","propagate","propagation","propel","propeller","prophecy","prophet","proposition","propulsion","prose","prospective","protend","proton","prototype","proverb"],"title":"The Boat Builder's Prototype","t":"In a small workshop near the river, a retired engineer named Mr. Huang has spent five years building a new kind of electric boat. His **prototype** is quiet, light and powered by solar panels. Instead of a traditional **propeller**, it uses a fish-like tail to **propel** itself through the water, a form of **propulsion** inspired by nature.\n\nMr. Huang's interest in engineering began in childhood. His father loved to quote an old **proverb**: \"A river is crossed one stone at a time.\" As a student, he studied physics and wrote a thesis on the behaviour of the **proton**. Later, he worked for a shipbuilding company, where he rose to **prominence** as a designer before receiving a **promotion** to chief engineer.\n\nHis idea was not popular at first. Investors called his **proposition** unrealistic, and some colleagues made a **prophecy** that it would fail. \"Everyone is a **prophet** when it comes to other people's failures,\" he jokes. He admits that he is **prone** to stubbornness, but he believes that patience can **prolong** a dream until it becomes reality.\n\nThe boat is beautifully designed. Its long, thin front seems to **protend** over the water like the neck of a swan. **Prospective** buyers, including tourist companies and fishermen, have begun to show interest. When a company offered to buy the design, Mr. Huang **promptly** refused. He wants to share it freely.\n\nHe hopes the technology will **propagate** quickly, and he has written a short book in clear, simple **prose** explaining how it works. He dislikes commercial **propaganda** and prefers honest explanations. \"The **propagation** of good ideas is the best gift we can give the future,\" he says."},"70":{"w":["provocative","provoke","provost","proximity","prudent","psychology","publicity","pulley","pumpkin","puppy","pyjamas","qualification","qualitative","quantify","quantitative","quartz","quench","quest","questionable","questionnaire"],"title":"The Happiness Survey","t":"Last spring, the psychology department at our university launched a research project on student happiness. Each student received a **questionnaire** asking about sleep, study, friendship and stress. The project's goal was to **quantify** something that many people think cannot be measured.\n\nThe research combined **quantitative** and **qualitative** methods. The questionnaire produced numbers, while interviews gave students the chance to explain their feelings in their own words. The project leader, Professor Yuan, said that **psychology** must use both approaches. \"Numbers tell us what happens,\" she said. \"Stories tell us why.\"\n\nSome results were surprising. Students who lived in close **proximity** to parks reported higher levels of happiness. Those with pets were especially cheerful; one student said that adopting a **puppy** had changed her life. Many students admitted that they studied in their **pyjamas** at night and rarely slept before two in the morning.\n\nThe project attracted a lot of **publicity**, and some findings were **provocative**. One newspaper headline claimed that \"Exams Make Students Miserable,\" which seemed designed to **provoke** anger. The university **provost** warned that the media had used a **questionable** interpretation of the data. Professor Yuan urged everyone to be **prudent** in drawing conclusions.\n\nThe research also had a playful side. In autumn, students organised a festival where they carved **pumpkins|pumpkin** and competed to lift weights using a simple rope and **pulley**. A geology student gave away small pieces of **quartz** as lucky charms, and volunteers served lemonade to **quench** everyone's thirst. Professor Yuan says that the **quest** for happiness is not a luxury. Many students said that good friends mattered more to them than any formal **qualification**."},"71":{"w":["quiver","radial","radiant","radiator","rainforest","raisin","rally","ramble","ranch","random","rap","rapidity","rapture","rascal","rash","rating","rattle","reactor","realistic","realization"],"title":"A Summer on the Ranch","t":"When I was sixteen, I spent a summer working on my uncle's **ranch** in Inner Mongolia. I had grown up in a city apartment, where the only heat came from a **radiator** under the window, and I knew nothing about horses or sheep. My parents hoped the experience would teach me to be less **rash** and more responsible.\n\nThe first weeks were hard. I was clumsy and slow, and my uncle called me a lazy **rascal** whenever I slept late. One morning, I made the **rash** decision to ride a young horse without permission. It threw me into the grass, and I lay there with my whole body beginning to **quiver** with shock. My uncle didn't shout; he simply helped me up and said, \"Next time, ask.\"\n\nSlowly, I came to love the work. I learned to recognise each sheep and to read the weather from the clouds. I was amazed at the **rapidity** with which storms could appear. At night, the wind would **rattle** the windows, and I would listen to my uncle **ramble** on about his youth. He kept a box of **raisins|raisin** in his pocket and gave them to the horses as treats.\n\nThe highlight of the summer was a local horse **rally**. Riders came from across the region, and spectators shouted with **rapture** as the horses raced across the grassland. Riders started in a **random** order drawn from a hat, and judges gave each one a **rating** for speed and style. A young herder even performed a **rap** song about the grassland that made everyone laugh.\n\nBy the end of the summer, I had come to a **realization**: life on the ranch was not easy, but it was honest. My uncle was **realistic** about the challenges: drought, falling prices and a plan to build a nuclear **reactor** on nearby grassland all made the future uncertain. On my last evening, he pointed to the fields, which spread out in a **radial** pattern around the well. \"Some people dream of the **rainforest** or the sea,\" he said. \"This is my world.\" His face was **radiant** in the sunset."},"72":{"w":["reassure","rebuke","recession","recipe","recipient","reciprocal","reckless","reckon","reclaim","reconcile","recruitment","rectangle","rectangular","rectify","recur","recurrence","recycle","redundant","reef","referee"],"title":"Saving the Reef","t":"Two years ago, my friend Mei lost her job in a bank during the economic **recession**. Like many others, she was made **redundant** with only a month's notice. Instead of looking for another office job, she joined a volunteer programme to protect a coral **reef** in the South China Sea.\n\nThe programme's **recruitment** process was strict. Volunteers needed diving skills and a doctor's certificate, and Mei spent months training. Her parents thought her decision was **reckless**, and her father even issued a stern **rebuke**. But Mei managed to **reassure** them that the work was safe and meaningful.\n\nThe reef had been badly damaged by pollution and illegal fishing. Volunteers divided it into **rectangular** sections, each marked by a **rectangle** of floating rope, and they replanted young coral in each area. They also collected plastic waste, which was later **recycled|recycle** into building materials. Over time, they began to **reclaim** parts of the reef that had been lost.\n\nProblems would **recur** constantly. Storms damaged new coral, and the **recurrence** of illegal fishing frustrated everyone. The volunteers worked with local fishermen to **rectify** the situation. They agreed on a **reciprocal** arrangement: fishermen would avoid protected areas, and in return, the programme would help them sell fish at higher prices. A local official acted as a **referee** in disputes.\n\nMei has also learned to cook. She shares a simple **recipe** for seaweed soup with every new volunteer. Last year, she was the **recipient** of a national award for environmental work. \"I **reckon** losing my job was the best thing that ever happened to me,\" she says. \"It helped me **reconcile** my love of nature with my need to work.\""},"73":{"w":["refinery","refrain","refugee","regenerate","regenerative","regime","regiment","regularity","reimburse","rejection","rejuvenate","relay","relegate","remainder","remnant","renaissance","renovation","repay","repeal","repel"],"title":"The Theatre Reborn","t":"In the centre of an old industrial city stands a theatre built in the 1920s. For decades it was the pride of the city, but when the local oil **refinery** closed, the economy collapsed and the theatre was abandoned. Only a **remnant** of its former beauty remained: a painted ceiling and a few velvet seats.\n\nThe theatre's rebirth began with a **refugee** named Mr. Haddad. He had fled his country after the old **regime** collapsed into civil war. In his youth, he had served in an army **regiment**, but his real love was music and drama. When he arrived in the city, he saw the empty theatre and decided to bring it back to life.\n\nHis first proposals met with **rejection**. City officials doubted that an unknown immigrant could manage such a project, and some tried to **relegate** it to the bottom of the budget list. But Mr. Haddad persisted. He raised money from local businesses and promised to **repay** every loan. Volunteers worked with great **regularity**, meeting every weekend to clean and repair the building.\n\nThe **renovation** took four years. Workers installed **regenerative** heating systems that reuse energy, and a new sound system to **relay** performances to screens in the square. The council finally agreed to **reimburse** part of the costs, and it voted to **repeal** an old law that had prevented cultural events in the area.\n\nToday, the theatre is full almost every night. Critics speak of a cultural **renaissance** in the city. The theatre seems to **rejuvenate** the neighbourhood, and new cafés and shops have opened nearby. Mr. Haddad says that art can **regenerate** a community. Young people now spend their evenings there instead of on the streets. The chorus of the theatre's first musical contains a simple **refrain**: \"We are home.\" Mr. Haddad has used the **remainder** of the funds to offer free tickets to students. He says the theatre is a place that will always **repel** hatred and welcome everyone."},"74":{"w":["repertoire","replacement","representation","reproduction","reptile","requisite","resemblance","resent","resentment","reservoir","reside","residential","residual","resolute","resonance","respectable","resultant","retard","retention","retirement"],"title":"The Keeper's Last Day","t":"Last month, Mr. Wu celebrated his **retirement** after forty years as a keeper in the **reptile** house of our city zoo. He knows every snake, lizard and tortoise by name, and his **repertoire** of stories about them seems endless. Children love him, and his departure was felt with great **resonance** throughout the zoo.\n\nHe became a keeper by chance. As a young man, he worked at a **reservoir** outside the city, where he often found snakes near the water. A local scientist noticed his calm way with animals and offered him a job. \"I didn't have the **requisite** qualifications,\" he says, \"but I had patience.\"\n\nHis work focused on the **reproduction** of endangered species. He helped breed rare turtles, whose numbers in the wild had fallen sharply. The **resultant** success made the zoo famous among scientists. He also cared for older animals, some of which had **residual** injuries from being kept illegally as pets.\n\nNot everyone appreciated his work. Some residents of the nearby **residential** area complained about the zoo's noise and smells, and Mr. Wu sometimes felt **resentment** at their criticism. But he did not **resent** them for long. \"People fear what they don't understand,\" he says. He worked to **retard** the spread of fear by teaching visitors about reptiles.\n\nMr. Wu is **resolute** in his belief that every animal deserves respect. He remembers a turtle whose shell bore a strange **resemblance** to a map of China. His **retention** of such details amazes his colleagues. His **replacement**, a young biologist, says she will try to continue his work. The zoo has commissioned a **representation** of him in bronze, which will **reside** in the reptile house forever. \"He was a **respectable** man,\" the director said, \"and a good friend.\""},"75":{"w":["retort","retrospect","reunion","revelation","revision","revive","revolve","ridicule","rig","rigorous","rim","rinse","riot","rip","ripple","ritual","roam","robust","romance","rooster"],"title":"Twenty Years Later","t":"Last summer, my secondary school class held a **reunion** in our old hometown. Twenty years had passed since graduation, and many of us had not seen each other since. The organiser, my friend Hao, prepared a careful plan, though he joked that it needed constant **revision** as more people joined.\n\nThe evening began with a **ritual** from our school days: everyone stood and sang the school song. Some voices were shaky, but the moment seemed to **revive** old memories. In **retrospect**, we all agreed that those years had shaped our lives more than we realised.\n\nAs the conversation began to **revolve** around old stories, there were many surprises. The biggest **revelation** came from Lin, the quietest girl in our class, who is now a famous engineer working on an offshore oil **rig**. When someone asked if she still remembered how the boys used to **ridicule** her, she gave a sharp **retort**: \"I remember. That's why I worked so hard.\"\n\nThere were funny stories too. Hao reminded us of the day a **rooster** wandered into the classroom during an exam, causing a small **riot** of laughter. Another classmate described how he had **ripped|rip** his trousers climbing over the school wall. Our old teacher, who was known for his **rigorous** standards, laughed until tears filled his eyes.\n\nLater, we walked by the lake where we used to **roam** after school. The water was still, and a stone sent a **ripple** across the surface. One couple revealed that their **romance** had begun at that very spot. Our teacher is now eighty but remains **robust** and cheerful. Before leaving, we filled our glasses to the **rim** and drank a toast. Then someone went to **rinse** the cups in the lake, just as we did when we were young."},"76":{"w":["roundabout","royalty","ruby","rupture","ruthless","safeguard","salmon","salvation","sanction","sardine","satisfactorily","saturate","saturation","savage","scarcity","scarlet","scoff","scorch","scramble","scrap"],"title":"The Fish Cannery","t":"On the northern coast, in a small town reached by a single **roundabout** off the highway, stands an old fish cannery. For a century, it has packed **salmon** and **sardines|sardine** caught by local boats. In its best years, the factory employed half the town, and the smell of fish was everywhere.\n\nThe business has faced **ruthless** competition. Large foreign companies have flooded the market with cheap products, and **saturation** of supermarket shelves has pushed prices down. At the same time, overfishing has caused a **scarcity** of salmon in the region. Some economists **scoff** at the idea that a small cannery can survive.\n\nProblems arrived one after another. During a **savage** winter storm, a water pipe suffered a **rupture**, flooding the warehouse. The next summer, a heatwave seemed to **scorch** the town, and rising temperatures in the sea pushed fish further north. Then international **sanctions|sanction** against a major trading partner closed one of the factory's best markets. Workers began to **scramble** for jobs elsewhere.\n\nThe owner, Mrs. Holm, refused to give up. She sold **scrap** metal from old machines to raise money and found new ways to **safeguard** the business. She introduced a premium brand of tinned salmon with a **scarlet** label, sold in high-end shops. A celebrity chef used it in a television programme, and sales rose quickly. She even signed a contract to pay **royalties|royalty** to a local artist whose painting appears on the tins.\n\nThe plan has worked **satisfactorily** so far. The factory now sells in fourteen countries, and a visiting member of the Danish **royalty** once praised its quality. Mrs. Holm keeps a small **ruby** brooch that belonged to her grandmother, who founded the company, and wears it at every business meeting. \"The cannery was our family's **salvation** during hard times,\" she says. \"Now it's my turn to save it.\" Her advice to other small businesses is simple: don't try to **saturate** the market; make something special."},"77":{"w":["script","scripture","scrub","scrutiny","sculpture","seam","seaside","sector","sedentary","seemingly","segregate","seminate","senator","sensation","senseless","sensitivity","sensor","sentiment","sermon","serpent"],"title":"The Seaside Sculptor","t":"In a small **seaside** town in the south, an artist named Mr. Lu creates huge **sculptures|sculpture** from driftwood and stones found on the beach. His most famous work, a giant **serpent** curving along the sand, attracts thousands of visitors each year. **Seemingly** simple, it took him three years to finish.\n\nMr. Lu's journey to art was unusual. For twenty years, he worked in the financial **sector**, leading a **sedentary** life behind a desk. He often felt that his work was **senseless**, though it paid well. One day, while walking on the beach, he had a strange **sensation** that the shapes of the driftwood were speaking to him. He left his job a month later.\n\nHis work is now the subject of serious **scrutiny** by critics. Some praise its **sensitivity** to nature; others call it too sentimental. Mr. Lu ignores the debate. \"**Sentiment** is not a weakness,\" he says. \"Art should touch the heart.\"\n\nHis sculptures have also become part of local culture. Every spring, a priest gives a **sermon** on the beach, reading from **scripture** about the creation of the world. A local **senator** has praised Mr. Lu for bringing tourism to the town. Mr. Lu has even written a short **script** for a children's play about the serpent, and he hopes it will help **seminate** a love of art in young people.\n\nHis methods are careful. He uses a brush to **scrub** each piece of wood clean, and he joins pieces together so that each **seam** is almost invisible. He installs a small **sensor** in each large work to measure wind and moisture, protecting it from damage. He refuses to **segregate** art from daily life. \"My sculptures belong to everyone,\" he says. \"Children can climb on them.\""},"78":{"w":["setback","shabby","shadowy","shaft","shaky","sham","shameful","shark","shatter","sheer","sheriff","shipbuilding","shipment","shipwreck","shortcut","shorten","shortfall","shorthand","shove","shovel"],"title":"The Shipyard Town","t":"On the rocky coast of Maine, in the northeastern United States, there is a small town famous for **shipbuilding**. For two hundred years, its shipyard produced fishing boats, cargo ships and even navy vessels. The town lies below **sheer** cliffs, and the harbour is said to be the safest on the coast.\n\nThe town's history includes many tragedies. A famous **shipwreck** in 1898 killed forty sailors, and old stories describe **sharks|shark** circling the broken ship. A museum displays a **shaky** photograph of the survivors, along with a **shovel** used to dig graves on the beach. In the 1920s, the town **sheriff** arrested a gang of smugglers who used a **shadowy** cave below the cliffs to hide their goods.\n\nIn recent decades, the shipyard suffered a serious **setback**. Orders fell, and the company faced a large financial **shortfall**. Some managers tried to **shorten** working hours and cut wages, and a few even tried a **sham** sale of the yard to avoid paying debts. Workers called it **shameful**. Many families left, and the town's main street became **shabby** and quiet.\n\nThe turning point came when a young engineer proposed building electric ferries. Her idea seemed to **shatter** old assumptions about what the yard could do. There was no **shortcut** to success: workers had to learn new skills, and the first ferry took three years to complete. The company had to **shove** old machines aside and install new equipment in the main **shaft** of the workshop.\n\nToday, the yard is busy again. Its first **shipment** of electric ferries left last spring, headed for Norway. The old foreman still takes notes in **shorthand** during meetings, a skill he learned sixty years ago. \"We nearly lost everything,\" he says. \"Now we're building the future.\""},"79":{"w":["shrewd","shrill","shrimp","shrine","shrub","shutter","siege","sift","signify","silicon","similarity","simulate","simultaneous","simultaneously","sincerity","siren","sitting-room","situated","skeleton","skeptical"],"title":"The Hidden Shrine","t":"In a quiet valley in the mountains of Gansu, archaeologists recently discovered an ancient **shrine** hidden beneath a field of **shrubs|shrub**. The site is **situated** near an old trading road, and experts believe it dates back more than a thousand years. Local farmers had passed it every day without knowing it was there.\n\nThe discovery began with a **shrewd** observation. A young archaeologist noticed that the plants in one area grew in strange straight lines, which might **signify** walls beneath the surface. When the team began to dig, they found stone foundations, painted pottery and the **skeleton** of a horse buried with great care.\n\nThe team worked slowly. They had to **sift** every handful of soil to find small objects such as coins and beads. They also used modern technology: drones equipped with **silicon** sensors scanned the ground, and computer programs helped to **simulate** what the shrine might have looked like. The team used several methods **simultaneously**, combining traditional digging with digital mapping.\n\nHistorians were at first **skeptical** about the site's age. But **similarity** between the pottery and pieces found in other ancient cities helped confirm the date. Written records describe a **siege** in the area in the eighth century, and some experts believe the shrine was abandoned during that war. The **simultaneous** discovery of weapons nearby supports this theory.\n\nLocal villagers have welcomed the project. One elderly farmer invited the team into his **sitting-room** and served dried **shrimp** and tea. He spoke with great **sincerity** about his pride in the discovery. Every evening, a **shrill** wind blows through the valley, and the farmer's grandson takes photos with an old camera, its **shutter** clicking softly. Once, an ambulance **siren** echoed through the hills when a worker fell, but he was not seriously hurt."},"80":{"w":["skip","skyscraper","slack","slander","slang","slap","slaughter","slim","slot","slumber","smuggle","snack","snail","snatch","sneak","sneer","sneeze","sniff","snob","snobbish"],"title":"The Snob from the City","t":"When my cousin Liang moved from Shanghai to a small mountain village to teach English, he arrived as a terrible **snob**. He had grown up in a flat on the fortieth floor of a **skyscraper**, and he looked down on everything about rural life. He would **sneer** at the villagers' clothes and **sniff** at the simple food, and his **snobbish** attitude made him few friends.\n\nHis first weeks were difficult. The village had no restaurants, only a small shop that sold **snacks|snack** and cigarettes. He was allergic to dust and began to **sneeze** every time he entered the classroom. The children laughed at him and taught him local **slang** that he did not understand. Some tried to **sneak** out of his lessons, and one boy would **snatch** his textbook and run away.\n\nThen something changed. During a rainstorm, Liang slipped on the muddy path, and an old farmer helped him up. The farmer invited him home, gave him dry clothes and let him **slumber** by the fire. When Liang woke, the farmer's wife had cooked a feast. They had even prepared a pig that was sent for **slaughter** that morning, to celebrate the New Year early.\n\nLiang's attitude began to soften. He stopped wanting to **skip** village events and started joining in. He learned how farmers protect their vegetables from every **snail** and insect. When he heard a rumour that a neighbour had tried to **smuggle** wild animals to the city, he refused to repeat it, knowing that such **slander** could ruin a family's reputation.\n\nToday, Liang is a popular teacher. He has grown **slim** from walking up and down the mountain every day, and he fills every free **slot** in his timetable with extra lessons. When he visits Shanghai, his friends tease him. Once, a friend gave him a playful **slap** on the back and asked if he was getting **slack**. \"Not at all,\" Liang said. \"I've never worked harder.\""},"81":{"w":["snore","soar","sociology","sodium","sofa","soften","softness","solidarity","solidify","solitary","solo","solvable","sophisticate","sorrowful","southwards","souvenir","sovereign","soy","spacious","spatial"],"title":"A Homestay in the South","t":"Last winter, as part of a **sociology** project, I spent a month living with an elderly couple in a fishing village in Fujian. My research topic was how small communities maintain **solidarity** as young people move away. I travelled **southwards** by train for two days, carrying a notebook and a bag of **souvenirs|souvenir** from Beijing as gifts.\n\nThe couple's house was surprisingly **spacious**, with a courtyard full of plants. Mr. and Mrs. Lin had lived there for fifty years. Every evening, Mr. Lin fell asleep on the old **sofa** after dinner and began to **snore** loudly, while his wife laughed and covered him with a blanket. Their love seemed to **soften** every hard moment of village life.\n\nMrs. Lin was an excellent cook. She made her own **soy** sauce, and she taught me that the best fish needs only a little salt. \"Too much **sodium** is bad for the heart,\" she warned. I admired the **softness** of her voice and her patience with my clumsy questions.\n\nThrough interviews, I learned that the village's problems were serious but **solvable**. Many elderly people lived **solitary** lives after their children left for the cities. But neighbours had formed a strong network of mutual help, which seemed to **solidify** over time. One old fisherman, who called himself a \"village **sophisticate**\" because he had once lived in Hong Kong, organised weekly gatherings. Another man played **solo** on a bamboo flute every evening, and his music made the whole village feel less **sorrowful**.\n\nI also studied the **spatial** layout of the village. Houses were built close together around a central temple, encouraging daily contact. Villagers spoke proudly of their history; in the past, the area had resisted foreign rule and defended its **sovereign** rights. On my last day, I watched seabirds **soar** above the harbour and felt sad to leave."},"82":{"w":["specifically","specification","spectacular","spectator","spectrum","speculate","spherical","spice","spinal","spiral","spokesman","spouse","squash","squat","stabilize","stagger","stainless","stairway","stalk","stall"],"title":"The Spiral Tower","t":"Three years ago, our city completed a **spectacular** new tower on the riverbank. Its design is a giant **spiral**, twisting upwards like a seashell, and at the top sits a **spherical** viewing room made of glass. Every evening, lights in every colour of the **spectrum** move across its surface, and thousands of **spectators|spectator** gather to watch.\n\nBuilding the tower was a huge challenge. Engineers had to follow strict **specifications|specification** for wind resistance, since the river valley is famous for strong storms. The frame is made of **stainless** steel, and special weights at the top help **stabilize** the structure. A long **stairway** winds around the inside for emergencies, though most visitors take the lift.\n\nThe project was not without problems. Work began to **stall** during the second year, when money ran short. Some businessmen began to **speculate** on nearby land, driving prices higher. A worker suffered a **spinal** injury after a fall, and the company was criticised for poor safety. The company's **spokesman** apologised publicly and promised to improve conditions.\n\nNot everyone loves the tower. Some residents say its cost would **stagger** anyone who saw the final bill. Others complain that it blocks the view of the old town. Critics also argue that the city should have **specifically** invested in housing and schools instead.\n\nBut the tower has brought life to the riverbank. Around its base, there is a busy market where traders sell **spice**, fruit and vegetables. One farmer brings a cart of giant **squash** every autumn. Children **squat** on the ground watching street performers, and couples stroll along the water. One old man, whose **spouse** died last year, comes every evening to watch the lights. \"It's like a flower on its **stalk**,\" he says. \"It reminds me of her garden.\""},"83":{"w":["stammer","standardize","stationary","stationery","steak","stereo","stereotype","stew","stiffness","stillness","stimulus","stockholder","stout","straighten","straightforward","strait","strangler","stray","streamline","stride"],"title":"The Café by the Strait","t":"On a small island across a narrow **strait** from the mainland, a woman named Mrs. Ko runs a busy café near the ferry pier. She is a **stout**, cheerful woman who greets every customer by name. Her menu is **straightforward**: beef **stew**, grilled **steak**, noodles and strong tea.\n\nMrs. Ko did not always run a café. For twenty years she was a **stockholder** in a large shipping company, until it was sold and she decided to change her life. She bought the café, cleaned it from top to bottom and installed an old **stereo** that plays jazz all day. She also sells **stationery** to students who come to study at the corner tables.\n\nAs a child, Mrs. Ko had a **stammer**, and classmates laughed at her. She says the experience taught her not to judge others by **stereotype**. Today, she welcomes everyone: fishermen, tourists, students and even a **stray** cat that sleeps by the door.\n\nThe café has its own rhythm. In the early morning, the island is full of **stillness**, and the boats in the harbour remain **stationary** until the tide turns. Then the first ferry arrives, and customers rush in. Mrs. Ko moves through the room with long, confident **strides|stride**, carrying trays of food.\n\nBehind the café grows an enormous fig tree known locally as a **strangler**, because its roots wrap around other trees. Mrs. Ko says the tree inspires her: \"It keeps growing, no matter what.\" Last year, the local government tried to **standardize** café menus for tourists, but she refused to **streamline** hers. She believes that good food is a **stimulus** for good conversation. After long days, she complains of **stiffness** in her back, but she simply **straightens|straighten** up and keeps working."},"84":{"w":["strife","striking","strive","stroll","stubborn","studio","stuffy","stun","sturdy","subdivide","subdue","subjective","subordinate","subscribe","subscript","subscription","subsequently","subsidiary","substantially","subtle"],"title":"The Photographer's Studio","t":"In a quiet street behind the market, there is a small photography **studio** run by an elderly man named Mr. Ren. Its windows are filled with **striking** black-and-white portraits of local people: farmers, teachers, children and soldiers. Many visitors **stroll** past without noticing, but those who step inside are often **stunned|stun** by the quality of the work.\n\nThe studio is old and a little **stuffy**, but it is full of history. Mr. Ren's father opened it in 1950, and it has survived years of political **strife** and economic change. The building itself is **sturdy**, with thick walls that have survived two earthquakes. Mr. Ren has **subdivided|subdivide** the back room into a darkroom and a storage space.\n\nMr. Ren is famously **stubborn**. He refuses to use digital cameras and insists on developing every photograph by hand. \"Photography is **subjective**,\" he says. \"A machine cannot decide what is beautiful.\" He believes that a good photographer must **strive** for honesty, capturing the **subtle** emotions in a person's face.\n\nOver the years, his business has changed **substantially**. In the 1980s, he had three **subordinate** staff and a **subsidiary** shop in the next town. **Subsequently**, the rise of phone cameras reduced his income, and he had to close the second shop. Today, he works alone. He relies on loyal customers who **subscribe** to an annual portrait service; the **subscription** includes one family photo each year.\n\nMr. Ren also teaches photography to children. In one lesson, he explained how chemical formulas work, writing a small **subscript** number beside each letter on the board. When a lively boy could not keep still, Mr. Ren managed to **subdue** him with a single raised eyebrow. \"Patience,\" he says, \"is the photographer's real camera.\""},"85":{"w":["successor","sue","suffice","suicide","suitcase","sullen","summon","superb","superficially","superiority","supersonic","superstition","supervise","supervision","supplementary","suppress","surge","surpass","susceptible","suspension"],"title":"Breaking the Sound Barrier","t":"When Captain Zhao was a boy, he dreamed of flying faster than sound. Today, at forty-five, he is a test pilot for a company developing a new **supersonic** passenger jet. The project aims to **surpass** the speed of any commercial aircraft in history, cutting the journey from Beijing to New York to just six hours.\n\nHis work is dangerous and demanding. Before each flight, engineers **supervise** every detail, and Captain Zhao undergoes strict medical checks. At high speeds, the body is **susceptible** to extreme pressure, and pilots must be in **superb** physical condition. He never packs more than a small **suitcase**, because he spends weeks at the test centre in the desert.\n\nThe project has had difficulties. Last year, a fault in the landing gear's **suspension** system caused a near accident, and the company had to halt tests for three months. A competitor tried to **sue** the company, claiming that its designs had been copied. Some investors said that continuing the project would be commercial **suicide**, and the company's share price fell sharply before a **surge** of new funding restored confidence.\n\nCaptain Zhao has strong views about safety. He refuses to **suppress** bad news and insists on honest reports. \"Looking at a problem **superficially** is not enough,\" he says. \"We must understand every cause.\" He keeps **supplementary** notes on every flight and shares them with younger pilots under his **supervision**.\n\nHe admits that pilots have their own **superstition**: he always wears the same old watch when he flies. He is training a younger colleague to be his **successor**. On days when the young pilot seems **sullen** or nervous, Captain Zhao will **summon** him for a quiet talk. \"Technical **superiority** is not enough,\" he tells him. \"Courage and humility must **suffice** when machines fail.\""},"86":{"w":["sweeten","sweetness","symmetrical","symmetry","symphony","symposium","symptom","synthesis","synthesize","systematically","tabulate","tack","tackle","tact","tactics","tangle","tanker","taper","tar","tariff"],"title":"The Sugar Scientist","t":"Dr. Fang is a food chemist who has spent ten years trying to **synthesize** a natural sugar substitute. Her goal is to **sweeten** food without the health risks of sugar. \"People love **sweetness**,\" she says, \"but too much sugar is making the world sick.\" Doctors often see the **symptoms|symptom** of diabetes in patients as young as twenty.\n\nHer research is careful and slow. She tests hundreds of plant compounds **systematically**, recording their taste, safety and cost. Her assistant must **tabulate** the results each week. The **synthesis** of a single promising compound can take months, and many experiments fail.\n\nLast year, she presented her findings at an international **symposium** in Geneva. Her talk attracted great interest, because she had found a compound that tastes almost exactly like sugar. One food company wanted to buy the rights immediately, but Dr. Fang used **tact** and patience in the negotiations. She knew that companies sometimes use aggressive **tactics** to buy research cheaply.\n\nThere were other problems to **tackle**. High import **tariffs|tariff** on certain plants made production expensive, and the plants had to be shipped across the ocean in refrigerated containers on a giant **tanker**. Regulations became a **tangle** of rules from different countries. Her team had to change **tack** several times before finding a solution.\n\nDr. Fang also cares about design. She works with a chocolate maker who creates **symmetrical** sweets shaped like flowers, which **taper** to a fine point. \"**Symmetry** makes food look more delicious,\" she explains. On weekends, she relaxes by listening to a **symphony** at the city concert hall. She laughs that her life is not as sweet as people imagine: on the road to her laboratory, workers are always spreading fresh **tar** to repair the potholes."},"87":{"w":["tease","telex","temperament","tempo","tenable","tensile","tentative","terminate","terminology","terrace","terrain","terrify","terrorist","testify","testimony","texture","thanksgiving","theft","theorem","therapy"],"title":"The Engineer's Testimony","t":"Five years ago, my uncle, a retired engineer, was asked to **testify** in a court case about the **theft** of steel cables from a construction site. The cables were made of high-**tensile** steel, used in bridges, and they were worth millions of yuan. His **testimony** helped prove that the thieves had inside help.\n\nMy uncle has a calm **temperament**, but the case made him nervous. Lawyers used complex legal **terminology**, and he struggled to follow the fast **tempo** of the questions. He offered only a **tentative** answer at first, then explained his reasoning clearly. \"The defence's argument was not **tenable**,\" he told me later. \"The cables could not have disappeared without someone opening the gate.\"\n\nThe case reminded him of his early career. In the 1980s, he worked for a company that sent orders to suppliers by **telex**, and messages sometimes took hours to arrive. He helped build roads across mountain **terrain** in the southwest, where storms could **terrify** even experienced workers. He still loves mathematics and enjoys explaining a geometry **theorem** to his grandchildren.\n\nAfter retirement, he suffered from back pain and began physical **therapy**. He now lives in a **terrace** house with a small garden, where he grows vegetables of every colour and **texture**. His grandchildren like to **tease** him about his old-fashioned habits, such as writing letters by hand.\n\nHe also enjoys films. His favourite is a thriller about a detective who stops a **terrorist** attack on a bridge, though he complains that the engineering details are wrong. Last year, an American friend invited him to celebrate **Thanksgiving**, and he gave a short speech about gratitude. He says that the court case was the last major event of his working life, and he was relieved when the judge decided to **terminate** the hearings after only two days."},"88":{"w":["thereafter","therein","thereof","thermal","thesis","thicken","thigh","thoroughly","thoughtless","thrash","thresh","threshold","thrifty","tickle","tighten","tightly","tile","tilt","timely","tiresome"],"title":"Harvest Days","t":"Every autumn during my childhood, I returned to my grandfather's village to help with the rice harvest. The work was hard. We cut the rice by hand, tied it **tightly** in bundles and carried it to the yard, where we would **thresh** it by beating the stalks against wooden frames. My **thighs|thigh** and arms ached for days afterwards.\n\nMy grandfather was a **thrifty** man who wasted nothing. He used the straw to make mats, fed the husks to the pigs and saved every grain. His house had a **tile** roof that leaked in heavy rain, and he repaired it himself every year. He taught me to **tighten** the ropes on the cart before every trip, so that the load would not **tilt** on the rough road.\n\nThe harvest was a race against the weather. A **timely** warning from the radio could save a whole crop from a storm. Once, a sudden rain fell before we finished, and the rice in the field was **thoroughly** soaked. My grandfather was not angry. \"**Thereafter**, we'll start earlier,\" he said simply.\n\nEvenings were the best part. My grandmother would **thicken** the soup with sweet potatoes, and we sat outside wearing **thermal** jackets against the cold. My little cousin would **tickle** me until I fell off the bench. Grandfather told stories, and **therein** lay the true magic of those days.\n\nYears later, I wrote my university **thesis** on rural farming traditions. My grandfather, though uneducated, read every word **thereof**. He said that young people are often **thoughtless** about where their food comes from. The village basketball team once **thrashed|thrash** a team from the city, a victory still remembered with pride. Standing on the **threshold** of his house, my grandfather always said goodbye with the same words: \"Farming is **tiresome**, but it feeds the world.\""},"89":{"w":["toad","token","tolerant","torment","torpedo","torque","touchable","tow","toxic","tract","trademark","trader","tradesman","tragic","trait","trample","tranquil","transaction","transcend","transient"],"title":"Life on the River","t":"For twenty years, my neighbour Mr. Gu has worked as a **trader** on the Yangtze River, buying and selling goods between towns. He owns a small boat that he uses to **tow** cargo barges, and he knows every bend of the river. He has a reputation as an honest **tradesman**, and his business card carries his own simple **trademark**: a small blue fish.\n\nMr. Gu has a calm **trait** that makes him popular with customers. He is **tolerant** of late payments and treats every **transaction** with care. He says that wealth is **transient**, but trust lasts forever. His boat's old engine has impressive **torque**, which allows it to pull heavy loads even against strong currents.\n\nThe river has a history that **transcends|transcend** any single life. Mr. Gu's father worked on the same waters, and he remembers wartime stories of a **torpedo** that sank a ship near the town. A small museum displays the remains. A huge **tract** of land along the riverbank was once covered with factories, and their **toxic** waste polluted the water for decades.\n\nNot all his memories are happy. Floods have caused **tragic** losses, and he still feels **torment** when he remembers a friend who drowned during a storm. Once, a crowd of tourists began to **trample** the riverbank gardens, and he had to ask them politely to move.\n\nToday, the river is cleaner and more **tranquil**. Fishermen have returned, and in summer the banks are full of frogs and **toads|toad**. Mr. Gu keeps a small copper **token** that his father gave him, believing it brings good luck. He says the river feels almost **touchable** in the morning mist, like a living friend."},"90":{"w":["transit","transition","transverse","traverse","tread","treasurer","tremendously","trench","triangular","tribute","trigger","triple","triumphant","trivial","trolley","tropic","trot","tuberculosis","tug","tulip"],"title":"The Tulip Garden","t":"On a hill outside the city, there is a famous garden where thousands of **tulips|tulip** bloom every spring. It was founded in the 1950s by a doctor who had recovered from **tuberculosis**. While resting in the mountains, he began growing flowers, and he believed they played a part in his recovery. The garden is now a **tribute** to his memory.\n\nVisitors arrive by public **transit**, and a small electric **trolley** carries elderly guests up the hill. The paths **traverse** the slope in long curves, and a **transverse** path cuts across the middle, dividing the garden into **triangular** sections. Each section shows a different colour of tulip, from deep red to pale yellow.\n\nThe garden's success has been **tremendously** important to the local economy. Visitor numbers have **tripled|triple** in the past decade, and the garden's **treasurer** reports a healthy profit. The money supports a training programme for young gardeners. One gardener explained how the **transition** from winter to spring is the most delicate time: a sudden frost can **trigger** damage to the bulbs.\n\nThe work is hard. Gardeners dig a deep **trench** around each bed to drain water, and they must **tread** carefully to avoid damaging young plants. Children love to **trot** behind the gardeners and help them **tug** weeds from the soil. Some visitors complain about **trivial** problems, such as the price of tea, but most are delighted.\n\nThe garden also has a greenhouse with plants from the **tropic** regions, including orchids and banana trees. At the end of each season, the staff hold a **triumphant** celebration, sharing food and music beneath the flowering trees."},"91":{"w":["tuna","turmoil","turnover","turtle","twilight","tyranny","tyrant","ultrasonic","ultraviolet","unanimous","unavoidable","unconventional","underestimate","underlying","undermine","underwear","undesirable","unemployment","unfit","unfold"],"title":"The Turtle Rescue Centre","t":"On a quiet beach in Hainan, a small rescue centre cares for injured sea **turtles|turtle**. Many arrive tangled in fishing nets meant for **tuna**, and others have swallowed plastic. The centre's founder, a former teacher named Ms. Luo, started the project fifteen years ago with no money and no training. \"People **underestimate** what ordinary volunteers can do,\" she says. On her first rescue, she rushed to the beach so quickly that she had packed nothing but **underwear** and a toothbrush.\n\nHer methods were **unconventional** from the start. She used old swimming pools as tanks and borrowed equipment from a local hospital. Vets now use **ultrasonic** scanners to check the turtles' health, and **ultraviolet** lamps keep the water clean. Each rescue story seems to **unfold** like a drama: some turtles recover quickly, while others need months of care.\n\nThe centre has faced difficult times. During a period of economic **turmoil**, donations fell sharply, and the staff **turnover** was high because many volunteers had to find paid work. Rising **unemployment** in the region made it hard to keep people. Some officials even tried to **undermine** the project, calling it an **undesirable** use of public land.\n\nMs. Luo believes that the **underlying** problem is human behaviour. Overfishing and pollution make some damage **unavoidable**, but much could be prevented. She gives talks in schools and speaks about how the island's history shaped attitudes to the sea. In the old days, a cruel local **tyrant** controlled the fishing trade, and his **tyranny** forced poor fishermen to take more fish than the sea could support.\n\nThe centre's work now enjoys **unanimous** support from the village council. Each release is a moving event. At **twilight**, volunteers carry the turtles to the water's edge and watch them swim away. Ms. Luo admits she is now **unfit** for long swims herself, but she still wades in to say goodbye."},"92":{"w":["uniformly","unify","universally","unlimited","unlock","unorthodox","unpaid","unprecedented","unreasonable","unreliable","untie","update","upgrade","uphold","uproar","uranium","utensil","utilization","vaccinate","validity"],"title":"The Mountain Vaccination Campaign","t":"Last spring, a team of young doctors set out to **vaccinate** every child in a remote mountain county against measles. The task was **unprecedented**: the county had never had a complete vaccination programme, and many villages could be reached only on foot. The team aimed to provide protection **uniformly** across the region, so that no child would be left out.\n\nMost doctors on the team were **unpaid** volunteers. They carried vaccines in cool boxes on their backs and slept in village schools. Because the hills contain old **uranium** mines, they also tested the drinking water in every village. Each evening, they would **update** their records on tablets, though the internet connection was **unreliable**. The government promised to **upgrade** mobile networks in the area, but progress was slow.\n\nThe doctors faced unexpected challenges. Some parents doubted the **validity** of the vaccines, influenced by rumours online. In one village, a meeting ended in **uproar** when a man accused the doctors of hiding dangerous side effects. The team leader, Dr. Shi, stayed calm. She explained that vaccines are **universally** recommended by doctors around the world and that refusing them was **unreasonable**. Slowly, trust grew.\n\nDr. Shi's methods were sometimes **unorthodox**. She invited children to **untie** ribbons on small gift boxes after each injection, and she used cooking **utensils|utensil** to demonstrate how germs spread. She believed that good health could **unify** communities that had long been divided.\n\nThe campaign was a success. Careful **utilization** of resources meant that no vaccine was wasted. The county now has **unlimited** access to free vaccines through a new clinic. Dr. Shi says the work helped her **uphold** the values that made her choose medicine. \"Knowledge,\" she says, \"is the key that can **unlock** a healthier future.\""},"93":{"w":["valve","vault","vector","vegetation","vein","vengeance","vent","ventilate","verbal","verdict","verge","versatile","verse","versus","veto","vicinity","vicious","vigor","villa","violate"],"title":"The Battle for the Villa","t":"On a hill above the lake stands an old **villa** built in the 1920s by a wealthy poet. Its walls are covered with ivy, and the gardens are full of wild **vegetation**. For years, the building stood empty, until a property developer announced plans to demolish it and build luxury apartments.\n\nThe plan caused fierce debate. Local residents formed a group to protect the villa, and the case went to court: \"City Heritage Society **versus** Golden Hill Developers.\" The residents argued that demolition would **violate** laws protecting historic buildings. The developer claimed the building was unsafe and on the **verge** of collapse.\n\nThe trial lasted months. Engineers examined every **valve** and pipe in the building and discovered that the old heating system could be repaired. They found a hidden **vault** beneath the house, full of the poet's letters and unpublished **verse**. One expert said that poor drainage had made the area a breeding ground for mosquitoes, which act as a **vector** for disease, but the residents promised to fix the problem.\n\nThe developer's lawyers were **vicious** in their attacks, accusing residents of acting out of **vengeance** because they had lost earlier disputes. The residents' lawyer, a **versatile** woman who also wrote novels, defended them with great **vigor**. Her **verbal** skill impressed everyone in the courtroom.\n\nFinally, the judge delivered her **verdict**: the villa must be preserved. The mayor tried to **veto** the decision, but the city council overruled him. Today, the villa is being restored. Workers **ventilate** the damp rooms, clean every **vent** and repair the marble floors, which have a beautiful blue **vein** running through them. Families living in the **vicinity** say the house has become the heart of the neighbourhood."},"94":{"w":["violation","violently","virgin","visa","viscous","visualize","vitamine","vividly","vocal","vocation","vocational","void","vow","vowel","vulgar","vulnerable","wade","wag","waitress","walnut"],"title":"The Vocational School","t":"Mr. Kang is the director of a **vocational** school in a mountain town, where young people learn practical skills such as cooking, mechanics and nursing. For him, teaching is not just a job but a **vocation**. \"Every student deserves a chance,\" he says, \"whatever their background.\"\n\nMany of his students come from **vulnerable** families. Some have left school early; others have parents working far away in the cities. Mr. Kang has made a **vow** never to turn a student away for lack of money. He is **vocal** in his criticism of policies that ignore rural youth, and he often writes to newspapers.\n\nThe school's courses are practical and lively. In the cooking class, students learn to make **walnut** cakes and soups, and they study the role of each **vitamine**, as the old textbook still spells it, in a healthy diet. In the English class, teachers focus on pronunciation, helping students to distinguish each **vowel** sound. One student, a young **waitress** who works evenings in a hotel, hopes to apply for a **visa** to work abroad.\n\nMr. Kang describes his own childhood **vividly**. He grew up beside a **virgin** forest, where he had to **wade** across a river to reach school. A stray dog would **wag** its tail and follow him every morning. He says that hard times taught him to **visualize** a better future.\n\nNot every moment is easy. Once, two students argued **violently** in the canteen, and Mr. Kang had to separate them. Using **vulgar** language is a **violation** of school rules, but he prefers conversation to punishment. \"Punishment leaves a **void**,\" he says. \"Understanding fills it.\" In the mechanics workshop, students learn to handle **viscous** oils and repair engines. Many graduates now run their own businesses."},"95":{"w":["ward","wardrobe","ware","warehouse","warfare","warning","warrant","wasp","wasteful","watchful","watertight","watery","watt","waver","weaver","web","wedge","wharf","whereby","whisker"],"title":"The Old Wharf","t":"Along the river in our city, an old **wharf** that once handled ships from around the world has become a busy cultural district. Its giant brick **warehouse**, built in the nineteenth century, now contains shops, galleries and cafés. Visitors stroll between stalls where traders sell local **wares|ware**, from pottery to handmade baskets.\n\nThe building has a long history. During the war, it was used to store weapons, and a small museum explains how modern **warfare** changed the city. Bombs damaged the roof, but the walls survived. After the war, the warehouse fell into decline, and the city eventually issued a **warrant** for its demolition.\n\nA group of artists saved it. They proposed a plan **whereby** the city would lease the building cheaply in exchange for repairs. The city's leaders seemed to **waver** at first, but they finally agreed. One of the artists was a **weaver** who set up her loom in a corner of the warehouse, and her colourful fabrics soon attracted visitors.\n\nThe renovation was careful. Workers made the roof **watertight** and installed lamps of just a few **watts|watt** each to save energy. They forced a wooden **wedge** under each old door to keep it from slamming. Volunteers removed a **wasp** nest from the attic, though they were **watchful** for stings. The artists also discouraged **wasteful** practices and recycled most of the old timber.\n\nToday, the wharf is full of life. A café serves **watery** but cheap coffee, which customers drink while watching the boats. A cat with long white **whiskers|whisker** sleeps on the steps, and a **web** of fairy lights hangs over the courtyard. Each room has its own **wardrobe** of costumes for theatre performances. Signs give a **warning** to visitors not to lean over the river railings. Children from a nearby hospital **ward** visit every week for art classes."},"96":{"w":["wholesome","wield","wilful","willow","winding","windmill","windy","wink","wither","withhold","witty","woe","woodpecker","workpiece","wrath","wrench","wrestle","wretched","wring","wrinkle"],"title":"The Windmill Village","t":"In the north of the Netherlands, there is a small village famous for its old **windmill**. The area is flat and **windy**, and for three centuries the mill ground grain for local farmers. A **winding** road leads to it through fields lined with **willow** trees, and a **woodpecker** can often be heard knocking on the branches.\n\nThe miller, an old man named Mr. de Vries, has worked there for fifty years. He is a **witty** storyteller who loves to **wink** at visitors and make them laugh. His face is full of **wrinkles|wrinkle**, but his hands are strong. He can still **wield** a heavy hammer and turn a giant **wrench** to adjust the machinery. Each wooden **workpiece** he makes for the mill is cut by hand.\n\nLife has not always been easy. During the war, the village suffered great **woe**, and many families lived in **wretched** conditions. Mr. de Vries remembers his mother trying to **wring** water from wet clothes by the fire because there was no soap. Later, when a company tried to buy the mill and turn it into a hotel, the villagers' **wrath** was enormous. They refused to sell.\n\nThe mill nearly closed ten years ago, when the government decided to **withhold** funding for historic buildings. Without money, the wooden sails began to **wither** and crack. Mr. de Vries had to **wrestle** with the decision to close, but young volunteers came to help him.\n\nToday, the mill produces flour again. Local bakers make **wholesome** bread from it, and visitors buy bags to take home. Mr. de Vries admits that he was a **wilful** child who never listened to his father. \"Now I know he was right,\" he says. \"This mill is our family's heart.\""},"97":{"w":["xerox","yacht","yearn","yeast","yielding","yoke","yolk","zinc"],"title":"Breakfast on the Yacht","t":"Last summer, my uncle invited our family to spend a weekend on his small **yacht**. He had dreamed of owning a boat for years, and he would **yearn** for the sea every time he sat in his office. When he finally retired, he bought an old sailing yacht and repaired it himself, replacing the rusty fittings with new **zinc** parts that resist salt water.\n\nOn the first morning, he made breakfast in the tiny kitchen. He baked bread with **yeast** he had brought from home, and he fried eggs with bright yellow **yolks|yolk**. We ate on deck as the sun rose, watching seabirds circle the boat.\n\nMy uncle is not a stubborn man; he has a **yielding** nature and listens to everyone's opinions. But on the boat, he is the captain, and he insists that everyone follow the rules. Before we left, he made us read a safety sheet, which he had copied on an old **xerox** machine at the harbour office.\n\nHe says that sailing frees him from the **yoke** of his old working life. \"For thirty years, I sat at a desk,\" he said. \"Now the wind decides where I go.\""}});
